/* GENERATED by tools/import_book.js from the finished manuscript of Book 2. Story text is the author's, verbatim; do not edit here. */
(function(){ G.HFQ.addSeriesText("htfa-2",{"chapters":[{"t":"Prologue — The Flag at Third Base","blocks":[{"p":"The flag above the baseball field looked ready to escape."},{"p":"It snapped straight out from its pole, folded around itself, then cracked open again. Across the diamond, somebody’s empty paper cup rolled from the bleachers into the dirt. Coach stepped on it before it could reach first base."},{"p":"“Burgess,” he called, “you watching the game or the weather?”"},{"p":"“Both.”"},{"p":"“Pick one.”"},{"p":"I looked at the runner on second. Then at the flag. Then back at the runner, who had apparently decided to steal third while I was conducting my personal meteorological survey."},{"p":"“Third!” I shouted."},{"p":"Our catcher threw. The ball sailed over the third baseman’s glove and bounced off the fence."},{"p":"The runner scored."},{"p":"I stood in right field and considered the possibility that Coach was right about a few things."},{"p":"It was early June, and I had been fourteen for twenty-six days. Fourteen sounded older than thirteen. It didn’t feel that way when my teammates looked at me as if I had invented errors."},{"p":"After the inning, I sat on the bench next to Theo Marshall, who was picking grass out of the stitching of his glove."},{"p":"“You didn’t make the throw,” he said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Then quit looking like you did.”"},{"p":"“The wind’s weird today.”"},{"p":"“The wind didn’t throw it either.”"},{"p":"He had a point. I hated when other people got there first."},{"p":"Dad was waiting beyond the fence after the game, his rectangular glasses sliding down his nose. He handed me a water bottle so cold it hurt my teeth."},{"p":"“You caught two fly balls,” he said."},{"p":"“And watched somebody steal a base.”"},{"p":"“That’s an improvement over last summer. Last summer you missed a fly ball because you watched an airplane.”"},{"p":"“I remember.”"},{"p":"“I was there.”"},{"p":"A gust lifted the corner of the score sheet clipped to Coach’s board. Dad caught it before it blew away."},{"p":"I watched the flag again. The pole stood still, but the fabric changed shape every second. Last summer, I’d learned that a pilot couldn’t make an airplane behave by gripping the controls harder. This summer, apparently, the air itself had decided to be difficult."},{"p":"My phone buzzed. A message from Imani lit the screen."},{"p":"*You still coming Saturday? Mara says bring your brain and a jacket.*"},{"p":"I typed back: *Can I bring just the jacket?*"},{"p":"Three dots appeared, vanished, and appeared again."},{"p":"*That would explain a lot.*"},{"p":"I smiled before I could help it."},{"p":"Dad tilted his head. “Good news?”"},{"p":"“Academy starts the summer sessions this weekend.”"},{"p":"“Ah.” He glanced at the flag. “Hope the weather cooperates.”"},{"p":"I looked at the flag too."},{"p":"I wasn’t sure wind knew how to cooperate."}]},{"t":"Chapter One — An Arrow on the Board","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing Beckett Shaw said when he saw me was, “You got taller.”"},{"p":"The second was, “Your hair didn’t.”"},{"p":"I had grown almost two inches since the previous summer, which meant my jeans were too short and my knees had become permanent hazards beneath the kitchen table. My dark auburn hair, however, continued to do whatever it wanted. I pushed it away from the little scar near my left eyebrow."},{"p":"“Good to see you too,” I said."},{"p":"Beckett was carrying a shoebox with holes punched in the lid."},{"p":"“What’s in there?”"},{"p":"“A thing.”"},{"p":"“A live thing?”"},{"p":"“Depends how you define alive.”"},{"p":"“Beckett.”"},{"p":"He lifted the lid. Inside was a small model airplane with a lopsided tail and a paper clip hanging from its nose."},{"p":"“It flies better with the clip,” he said."},{"p":"“Does it?”"},{"p":"“No. But it crashes more consistently.”"},{"p":"That was Beckett. Six failures meant six data points."},{"p":"The academy classroom at Hampton Roads Executive Airport smelled of dry-erase markers and the coffee someone had spilled near the door. The ceiling fan ticked every third turn. Beyond the windows, a white Cessna sat on the ramp with its wings level and its nose pointed into the morning sun."},{"p":"Imani Bellweather had already claimed a seat near the front. Her black braids were gathered at the back of her head, and she was drawing neat little arrows in a notebook. Rafe Solis sat beside her, turning a pen between his fingers like a tiny propeller."},{"p":"“You’re late,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“I’m six minutes early.”"},{"p":"“Exactly. I expected nine.”"},{"p":"He grinned. His dimples appeared. Rafe had grown broader across the shoulders and started making air traffic controller announcements whenever he thought no adults were listening."},{"p":"“Attention, arriving passenger,” he said. “Please secure all loose—”"},{"p":"“Mouths,” Imani supplied."},{"p":"Rafe closed his."},{"p":"Mara Ellery came in carrying a stack of folders and a rolled-up airport diagram. She looked exactly as she had the previous summer: dark blond hair in a low ponytail, pale blue eyes that noticed everything, and a way of moving through a room that made you straighten up without knowing why."},{"p":"“Welcome back,” she said. “Nobody has to tell me what they did on vacation. You can tell each other at lunch. Today we’re talking about wind.”"},{"p":"Beckett raised his hand."},{"p":"“Yes?”"},{"p":"“Is the fan part of the demonstration?”"},{"p":"“No. The fan is part of a long-running argument with the building manager.”"},{"p":"She wrote three words across the board: **DIRECTION. SPEED. GUSTS.**"},{"p":"Then she drew a runway as two parallel lines."},{"p":"“Let’s say an airplane is preparing to land here. The runway points north and south. The wind is coming from the west. What changes?”"},{"p":"“The plane gets pushed east,” I said."},{"p":"Mara turned toward me. “Maybe. But that answer skips a few things. Let’s start with what the pilot is trying to do.”"},{"p":"“Land on the runway.”"},{"p":"“Yes. More specifically, maintain the proper approach path and alignment while managing the wind. A crosswind makes that more complicated.”"},{"p":"She drew an arrow from the left side of the runway toward the right."},{"p":"“Wind is named for the direction it comes *from*. A west wind comes from the west. Not toward it.”"},{"p":"Rafe lifted a finger. “My uncle says that’s backward.”"},{"p":"“Your uncle is welcome to write a strongly worded letter to the atmosphere.”"},{"p":"I laughed. So did everyone else, even Imani, though she covered her mouth with the back of her hand."},{"p":"Mara drew a second arrow, this one along the runway."},{"p":"“A headwind generally helps reduce the groundspeed needed for a given airspeed on approach. A tailwind does the opposite. A crosswind introduces sideways drift that the pilot must account for. Sometimes the wind is a combination of those components.”"},{"p":"She paused, marker still in her hand."},{"p":"“Before anyone starts doing math, what’s the difference between airspeed and groundspeed?”"},{"p":"I knew this one. At least I thought I did."},{"p":"“Airspeed is how fast the airplane goes. Groundspeed is how fast it goes over the ground.”"},{"p":"“Close enough to build on. Airspeed is speed relative to the surrounding air. Groundspeed is speed relative to the ground. Wind can make those numbers quite different.”"},{"p":"She slid a laminated chart across the desk: a little airplane, a compass rose, and arrows scattered like dropped pencils."},{"p":"“Today we’ll use simple examples. Nobody is calculating a landing limit from memory and calling it a flight plan. Actual decisions depend on the airplane, conditions, procedures, and the pilot’s abilities.”"},{"p":"Beckett held up his model."},{"p":"“Can I demonstrate with this?”"},{"p":"“Does it have a working engine?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then we’re not going to call it a powered-airplane demonstration. But you may use it to help visualize the directions.”"},{"p":"He stood beside the board, pointed the model’s nose along the runway, and made a noise like a lawn mower failing to start."},{"p":"“That’s not necessary,” Mara said."},{"p":"“It helps me.”"},{"p":"“Quietly, then.”"},{"p":"He tried a quieter engine noise. It sounded like a mosquito in a jar."},{"p":"We spent the next half hour moving paper airplanes across the diagram and trying to identify where the wind would be coming from. Imani got every direction right. Rafe mixed up east and west twice and blamed the room. Beckett’s model lost its paper clip and nearly took out Mara’s coffee."},{"p":"I answered most of the questions correctly. That was the problem. Each right answer made me more eager to beat everyone to the next one."},{"p":"Then Mara changed the picture."},{"p":"She drew a wind arrow angled across the runway rather than straight across it."},{"p":"“Lincoln. Is this a pure crosswind?”"},{"p":"“No. It’s partly a headwind.”"},{"p":"“Good. Would you expect the crosswind component to be the same as the full wind speed?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth."},{"p":"Nothing came out."},{"p":"I could see the arrow. I knew what it meant. The words, apparently, had gone on break."},{"p":"“Because only some of it is sideways?” I said finally."},{"p":"“Exactly. We can break the wind into components. You don’t need to do that in your head in a hurry. You need to understand what the numbers mean and know where to find reliable information.”"},{"p":"She turned back to the board."},{"p":"I glanced at Imani. She wasn’t looking at me. She had drawn the angled arrow and split it into two smaller ones, one along the runway and one across it. I copied her drawing, then added labels."},{"p":"At break, we took our lunches outside to a picnic table beneath a strip of shade. The air smelled of cut grass and warm asphalt. Somewhere behind the hangars, an engine started, coughed once, then settled into a steady hum."},{"p":"“You answered that last one right,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Eventually.”"},{"p":"“Eventually counts.”"},{"p":"“Not if I freeze like that in an airplane.”"},{"p":"She stopped peeling her orange. “You were standing in a classroom.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Then maybe don’t turn it into an emergency.”"},{"p":"Rafe pointed his sandwich at me. “I have frozen in a classroom plenty of times. It’s one of my main talents.”"},{"p":"Beckett set his shoebox on the table and took out the model. The paper clip was missing."},{"p":"“Did anybody see where the weight went?” he asked."},{"p":"“You mean the thing you launched toward Mara’s coffee?” I said."},{"p":"“It didn’t launch. It separated.”"},{"p":"“That’s worse.”"},{"p":"He bent to search beneath the table. Rafe joined him. Within thirty seconds, both of them were crawling around on the concrete while Imani and I tried not to laugh."},{"p":"A sudden gust rattled the leaves overhead. One of Imani’s loose worksheets slid off the table. I grabbed for it and missed. It sailed across the walkway, turned once, and lodged against the leg of a trash can."},{"p":"“Don’t chase it into the ramp,” Mara called from the doorway."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to!” I shouted."},{"p":"I absolutely had."},{"p":"Mara walked over, picked up the sheet, and handed it back."},{"p":"“Wind’s doing a fine job of teaching today,” she said."},{"p":"I looked down at the diagram. The arrows were neat and simple. Outside, the wind wasn’t. It came in little punches, then went quiet, then moved the leaves in a different direction."},{"p":"“Is it always this messy?” I asked."},{"p":"“Sometimes it’s messier.”"},{"p":"She glanced toward the windsock beyond the fence."},{"p":"“That’s why we pay attention before we fly. And keep paying attention after we’ve decided.”"},{"p":"I watched the windsock lift, sag, and lift again."},{"p":"For the first time that morning, I didn’t try to guess what Mara would say next."}]},{"t":"Chapter Two — A Seat in the Back","blocks":[{"p":"By Thursday, I’d checked the weekend forecast so often Mom asked whether the weather app was paying me."},{"p":"“Just looking,” I said."},{"p":"“You’ve been just looking through breakfast, lunch, and dinner.”"},{"p":"“The wind keeps changing.”"},{"p":"“Imagine that.”"},{"p":"Mom was standing at the kitchen counter in her work clothes, one shoe on and one shoe off, trying to find the lid to her travel mug. She worked in municipal records, which apparently involved a lot of paper and an unreasonable number of people who couldn’t remember where they’d filed things."},{"p":"Dad sat at the table repairing the loose lace on his baseball glove with a needle that looked too large for the job."},{"p":"“Forecasts change,” he said. “You know that.”"},{"p":"“I’m checking whether Saturday’s flight might get canceled.”"},{"p":"“Will checking more often keep it from getting canceled?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then the forecast has an advantage over you.”"},{"p":"Mom found the mug lid inside the refrigerator. Nobody knew how it had gotten there. Dad suggested she file a report."},{"p":"I put my phone face down."},{"p":"Saturday wasn’t supposed to be my flight anyway. Mara had scheduled a ground lesson and a short supervised observation opportunity, depending on conditions and staffing. I knew the difference. It still felt like standing outside a bakery with no money."},{"p":"On Friday evening, Uncle Everett stopped by carrying a paper grocery bag that clinked when he set it on the counter."},{"p":"“What’s that?” I asked."},{"p":"“Pickles.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Your father asked for them.”"},{"p":"“I asked for one jar,” Dad said from the living room."},{"p":"“There were six on sale.”"},{"p":"Everett had spent enough years in the Navy to believe that buying supplies in small quantities was a personal failing. An old shoulder injury made him use his other arm for high shelves. He never mentioned it unless someone offered to help."},{"p":"He found me staring at the forecast after dinner."},{"p":"“Planning a picnic?” he asked."},{"p":"“Academy.”"},{"p":"“Ah.”"},{"p":"He pulled out a chair. “What’s bothering you?”"},{"p":"“Nothing.”"},{"p":"“Good. Then you won’t mind explaining why you look as though the phone owes you money.”"},{"p":"I turned the screen toward him. The forecast showed gusts well above the sustained wind and arrows shifting through the afternoon."},{"p":"“I don’t know whether we’ll fly.”"},{"p":"“Neither do I.”"},{"p":"“You didn’t even look.”"},{"p":"“Looking wouldn’t tell me what the conditions will actually be when you get there.”"},{"p":"I frowned. “It tells you something.”"},{"p":"“Of course. A forecast is useful. It isn’t a guarantee. The people responsible for the flight will look at current conditions too, and they’ll make a decision based on more than a number on your phone.”"},{"p":"I traced one of the arrows with my thumb."},{"p":"“Were you ever scared of crosswinds?”"},{"p":"He leaned back. “I was scared of plenty of things.”"},{"p":"“You flew fighter jets.”"},{"p":"“Those airplanes didn’t make me immune to bad weather. They made it important to respect it.”"},{"p":"He rubbed the edge of the table with one thumb, as though checking for a rough spot."},{"p":"“When I was learning, I wanted every landing to look perfect. That wasn’t always a helpful goal. Sometimes the useful lesson was that the approach wasn’t right, and you needed to stop trying to rescue it.”"},{"p":"“Like going around?”"},{"p":"“Exactly. A go-around is a normal option when a landing shouldn’t be continued. Your instructor will explain the procedures for the airplane you’re flying. But the judgment starts with recognizing that you have a choice.”"},{"p":"I looked at the forecast again."},{"p":"“So if it’s too windy, Mara cancels.”"},{"p":"“Or changes the lesson. Or waits. I don’t know what she’ll decide. That’s her job, not yours.”"},{"p":"I put the phone away. Everett hadn’t solved anything, but refreshing the screen wouldn’t either."},{"p":"The next morning, the sky over Chesapeake was pale and hazy. Dad drove me to the airport with the windows cracked because the air conditioner had begun making a noise like a drawer full of silverware."},{"p":"At the academy building, Rafe was already talking to a younger student named Owen, who had a bright red backpack and looked as if he’d rather be anywhere else."},{"p":"“Lincoln!” Rafe called. “Tell him I’m not making up the radio alphabet.”"},{"p":"“You’re probably making up half of it,” I said."},{"p":"“Alfa, Bravo, Charlie,” Rafe began."},{"p":"“See?” I told Owen. “He knows three.”"},{"p":"Owen smiled a little."},{"p":"Inside, Mara had posted the morning weather information on the board. She’d also written two words beneath it: **PLAN B**."},{"p":"“Good morning,” she said. “We have enough gusty wind to change what we’re doing. No student flying session this morning.”"},{"p":"A small, unpleasant feeling settled beneath my ribs. I had known this might happen. Knowing didn’t make it fun."},{"p":"Beckett looked at the board. “What’s Plan B?”"},{"p":"“An observation exercise. We’ll stay on the ground, talk through how pilots evaluate wind, and practice reading the windsock and airport weather reports. Then we’ll use a simulator to look at drift. Nobody’s getting a prize for pretending today is a perfect flying day.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “Is there a prize for knowing it isn’t?”"},{"p":"“Yes,” Mara said. “You get to keep learning.”"},{"p":"“Terrible prize.”"},{"p":"“Nonrefundable.”"},{"p":"We walked out to a designated observation area away from the movement surfaces. Mara made sure Owen stayed with the group. Beyond the fence, a windsock pointed nearly straight out, then sagged, then snapped sideways as another gust passed through."},{"p":"Mara asked us to describe what we saw without deciding too quickly what it meant."},{"p":"“Wind from the left,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Stronger now,” Imani added."},{"p":"“Not steady,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. What can’t you tell from watching the sock alone?”"},{"p":"“The exact speed?” I said."},{"p":"“Right. A windsock gives useful visual clues, not a complete weather briefing.”"},{"p":"We listened to the wind press through the chain-link fence. It sounded like someone brushing a broom across a wire screen."},{"p":"Owen raised his hand halfway, then lowered it."},{"p":"Mara noticed. “Go ahead.”"},{"p":"“Do pilots get in trouble if they don’t land?” he asked."},{"p":"Rafe stopped fidgeting with his pen. Beckett looked up from his notebook."},{"p":"“No,” Mara said. “Choosing not to continue an approach when it isn’t safe is part of good flying. There are procedures and decisions involved, but the idea is simple: a landing isn’t something you have to force.”"},{"p":"Owen nodded, though I wasn’t sure he believed her yet."},{"p":"I remembered my first summer, when a canceled flight had felt like someone had taken a present away from me. Now I was disappointed again, but I could see the windsock twisting in front of us. I could feel the gusts tugging at my shirt."},{"p":"Mara paired us for the simulator exercise. Imani and I worked together while Beckett and Rafe argued over which of them was better at following directions. The simulator showed an airplane on a long approach to a runway. Mara explained that it was a simplified exercise, not a substitute for flight training, and that she would handle the controls while we observed the ground track."},{"p":"“Where is the nose pointing?” she asked."},{"p":"“Left of the runway,” I said."},{"p":"“And where is the airplane actually moving?”"},{"p":"I watched the picture for another second."},{"p":"“Toward the runway.”"},{"p":"“That’s the distinction I want you to notice. Heading and ground track are not always the same when wind is involved.”"},{"p":"Imani leaned forward. “It’s like walking across a moving walkway sideways.”"},{"p":"“Or swimming across a river with a current,” Mara said. “The details aren’t identical, but the idea of compensating for movement of the surrounding medium can help.”"},{"p":"On the screen, the airplane continued toward the runway even though its nose wasn’t pointed exactly down the centerline."},{"p":"“Does it land like that?” Owen asked from behind us."},{"p":"“Pilots use specific techniques to manage crosswinds during approach and landing,” Mara said. “We’ll study those carefully later. For now, we’re watching how wind affects the path over the ground.”"},{"p":"I was about to ask whether I could try the controls when the simulator picture jerked sideways. Rafe had bumped the table with his hip."},{"p":"“Wind shear,” he announced."},{"p":"“Rafe,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Furniture shear?”"},{"p":"“Move your hip.”"},{"p":"He did."},{"p":"By lunchtime, the gusts were still rattling the windows. I hadn’t flown. I hadn’t even touched a yoke. But my notebook was full of arrows and observations that made more sense than the ones I’d drawn at the start of the week."},{"p":"Owen sat beside me with his red backpack on his knees."},{"p":"“Are you disappointed?” he asked."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said."},{"p":"“Me too.”"},{"p":"I thought about telling him that a good pilot never minded a canceled flight. That sounded like the kind of thing an adult might say in a poster. It also wasn’t true."},{"p":"“I’m still glad Mara made the call,” I said instead."},{"p":"He considered that. “Can you be both?”"},{"p":"“Apparently.”"},{"p":"Outside, the windsock kicked sideways again. I watched it for a moment, then went back to eating my sandwich before the wind found a way to steal that too."}]},{"t":"Chapter Three — The Wrong Side of the Lake","blocks":[{"p":"The first sign that our trip to Michigan was going to be complicated was a map on the refrigerator."},{"p":"Dad had printed it at the library. It showed the eastern half of the United States in gray lines, with a thick blue route running from Virginia Beach to Houghton Lake. He had taped it beside the grocery list, directly over a magnet shaped like a crab."},{"p":"I stared at it while I ate cereal."},{"p":"“That’s a lot of road,” I said."},{"p":"“Excellent observation,” Dad said. “Have you considered a career in cartography?”"},{"p":"“Why are we driving?”"},{"p":"“Because your mother and I have reviewed the family budget and discovered that our private jet is still imaginary.”"},{"p":"Mom walked in carrying a basket of laundry. “And because we want to stop along the way. Your father has already found a historic bridge, a lighthouse, and a diner he insists serves the world’s second-best pie.”"},{"p":"“Why second-best?”"},{"p":"“He says he hasn’t tried it yet.”"},{"p":"Dad nodded. “I believe in leaving room for improvement.”"},{"p":"I pushed a soggy cereal square around the bowl. We were visiting Mom’s old college friend and her family near Houghton Lake. Their cabin sat a little way back from the shore, and they had promised fishing, swimming, and a boat with an outboard motor that worked most of the time."},{"p":"I had looked up the lake the night before. It was enormous. The shape on my screen looked like somebody had dropped a lumpy pancake into the middle of Michigan."},{"p":"What I hadn’t looked up was how far it was from the airport."},{"p":"“I’m missing a Saturday at the academy,” I said."},{"p":"Mom set down the laundry basket. “You’re missing one Saturday. We talked to Mara.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Then what’s the problem?”"},{"p":"I glanced at the map. “Everybody else gets ahead.”"},{"p":"Dad took off his glasses. “Lincoln, it isn’t a race.”"},{"p":"“It’s easy to say that when you aren’t the one trying to learn.”"},{"p":"He went quiet. That was worse than when he made a joke."},{"p":"Mom took a breath, then picked up the basket again. “Finish breakfast. We’re leaving in twenty minutes, and your father still has to decide which six books are absolutely necessary for a road trip.”"},{"p":"“Four,” Dad said."},{"p":"“Six,” she replied."},{"p":"I checked my phone. Imani had sent a picture of her notebook. She’d drawn a little airplane over a map and labeled three arrows: AIRSPEED, WIND, and GROUND TRACK."},{"p":"*I made you a study sheet for the trip,* she’d written. *You can complain after you learn it.*"},{"p":"I typed: *I am extremely talented at doing both.*"},{"p":"Her reply came immediately. *We know.*"},{"p":"I packed the notebook."},{"p":"By the time we reached Pennsylvania, the inside of our car smelled like pretzels, coffee, and the orange peels Dad had been collecting in a paper bag for reasons known only to him. Mom drove the first stretch. Dad navigated, which mostly meant reading historical facts aloud until she threatened to leave him at a rest stop."},{"p":"Somewhere beyond the mountains, a line of dark clouds rose along the horizon."},{"p":"“Rain?” I asked."},{"p":"“Looks like it,” Mom said."},{"p":"I opened the weather app."},{"p":"“Don’t turn this into an air traffic control center,” Dad said from the passenger seat."},{"p":"“I’m checking.”"},{"p":"“I can see that.”"},{"p":"The radar showed a band of showers moving across the highway ahead of us. I knew a little more about weather than I had a year ago, which made me more curious and, unfortunately, much more annoying."},{"p":"“We could stop before it,” I said."},{"p":"“Maybe,” Mom said."},{"p":"“Or take the road north.”"},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"“Because the storms are moving east and—”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"I stopped."},{"p":"Mom kept her eyes on the road. “I appreciate the information. I really do. But I’m driving, and I’m watching the road. Tell me what you see once, then let me decide what to do with it.”"},{"p":"I looked at the screen again. The colored shapes seemed so definite. Red, yellow, green. Like the weather had agreed to organize itself into neat boxes for my convenience."},{"p":"“There’s rain ahead,” I said. “The app doesn’t tell me exactly what it’ll be like when we get there.”"},{"p":"“Thank you.”"},{"p":"We stopped for dinner while the worst of the rain passed. Dad’s famous pie diner turned out to be a gas station with a sandwich counter. The pie was in a plastic container."},{"p":"“Second-best,” I said, looking at it."},{"p":"“Potentially,” Dad replied."},{"p":"We reached Houghton Lake the following afternoon, after a night in a hotel where the vending machine ate my dollar and the ice machine sounded like it was building a house."},{"p":"The cabin belonged to Mom’s friend, Dana Mercer, and her husband, Pete. They had a daughter named Hazel who was fourteen like me and apparently knew how to do everything involving water."},{"p":"She met us on the porch wearing an old life jacket over a T-shirt."},{"p":"“You’re Lincoln?” she asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Mom says you fly airplanes.”"},{"p":"“Not by myself.”"},{"p":"“Oh.” She looked disappointed for half a second, then recovered. “Can you drive a boat?”"},{"p":"“Also not by myself.”"},{"p":"“Excellent. We have a lot in common.”"},{"p":"She led me down a sandy path toward the shore."},{"p":"The lake spread out farther than I expected, blue-gray under a bright sky. Wind pushed little ridges across the surface. Farther out, the water flashed silver where the sun caught the tops of the waves."},{"p":"A narrow aluminum fishing boat bumped against a dock. Its bow line pulled tight, slackened, and pulled tight again."},{"p":"Hazel pointed toward a small island-shaped clump of trees in the distance."},{"p":"“That’s where we’re going tomorrow if the wind settles down.”"},{"p":"“Why if?” I asked."},{"p":"She gave me a look. “Because it’s a little boat and a big lake.”"},{"p":"I watched the boat’s bow rise and fall."},{"p":"“What if it doesn’t settle?”"},{"p":"“Then we don’t go.”"},{"p":"She said it like there was nothing else to discuss."},{"p":"I remembered how disappointed I’d been when Mara canceled the flying session, even though I understood why. Hazel had just described canceling something she clearly wanted to do, and she didn’t seem bothered."},{"p":"Then a wave slapped the dock and sprayed her shoes."},{"p":"“Okay,” she said. “I’m a little bothered.”"},{"p":"That made more sense."},{"p":"We spent the evening skipping stones. Hazel could make hers bounce five or six times. Mine sank with the confidence of bricks."},{"p":"“Too much arm,” she told me after my eighth failure."},{"p":"“I’ve heard that before.”"},{"p":"“From who?”"},{"p":"“My flight instructor.”"},{"p":"Hazel looked at the stone in my hand. “She teaches rock skipping?”"},{"p":"“No. She teaches me not to fight things that don’t need fighting.”"},{"p":"“That sounds exhausting.”"},{"p":"“It is.”"},{"p":"I picked a flatter stone and threw it with less force. It bounced twice before disappearing."},{"p":"Hazel held up two fingers. “A personal best.”"},{"p":"I grinned, then reached for another stone."},{"p":"Behind us, the wind shifted. The waves began coming toward the dock at a different angle. I watched them for a while without saying anything."},{"p":"For once, I didn’t need to be the first person to explain what they meant."}]},{"t":"Chapter Four — The Boat That Stayed Tied","blocks":[{"p":"The next morning, Hazel knocked on the cabin door at seven."},{"p":"I know because I looked at the clock before I decided I was still asleep."},{"p":"“Your friend is here,” Dad called from the kitchen."},{"p":"“She’s not my friend,” I said into the pillow."},{"p":"“She brought donuts.”"},{"p":"I got up."},{"p":"Outside, the air was cool enough to raise goose bumps on my arms. A few clouds were scattered over the lake, and the water looked calmer close to shore. Hazel was sitting on the porch steps eating a powdered donut with the concentration of someone solving a difficult problem."},{"p":"“Pete says we might take the boat out,” she said."},{"p":"“Great.”"},{"p":"“Might.”"},{"p":"“Got it.”"},{"p":"She handed me a donut. I bit into it and immediately covered my shirt in white powder."},{"p":"“Professional,” she said."},{"p":"“I trained for years.”"},{"p":"Pete was already down at the dock, checking the boat. He was broad-shouldered, with sunburned forearms and a cap that had probably been blue once. A small orange tackle box sat beside him."},{"p":"“Morning,” he said. “Before anybody gets excited, I want to see what the wind does.”"},{"p":"Hazel rolled her eyes, but she didn’t argue."},{"p":"I glanced at the water. “It looks okay.”"},{"p":"“From here,” Pete said. “That’s part of the problem.”"},{"p":"He pointed across the lake. Out past the shelter of the trees, the surface was rougher. Darker streaks ran across it, and little white tips appeared on some of the waves."},{"p":"“The shore blocks some of the wind,” he said. “Get out there, and it’s a different story.”"},{"p":"I thought about the academy observation area. We could see the windsock, but Mara still checked weather reports and other information. One clue wasn’t the whole story."},{"p":"Pete lifted a life jacket from the boat. “And if we go, everyone wears one that fits. No exceptions.”"},{"p":"Hazel grabbed hers. “I know.”"},{"p":"“Then you won’t mind hearing it again.”"},{"p":"I tried on a jacket that was too large. Pete traded it for a smaller one and checked the fit. He looked at the boat’s fuel supply, the dock lines, and the small motor. I didn’t understand every check, but I recognized the routine."},{"p":"“Like a preflight,” I said."},{"p":"Pete glanced over. “What’s that?”"},{"p":"“Checking the airplane before you go. Not because you think something’s wrong. Because you want to find out if something is.”"},{"p":"“That’s a decent way to put it.”"},{"p":"Hazel leaned toward me. “Don’t encourage him. He already checks everything twice.”"},{"p":"“Only twice?” Pete said. “I’m slipping.”"},{"p":"For another twenty minutes, we sat on the dock while Pete watched the water and checked a local marine forecast on his phone. The wind was expected to strengthen through the morning."},{"p":"At last he stood and set the life jackets back inside the boat."},{"p":"“Not today.”"},{"p":"Hazel made a sound like someone had stepped on her foot."},{"p":"“Seriously?”"},{"p":"“Seriously.”"},{"p":"“It’s barely moving here.”"},{"p":"“Here isn’t out there.”"},{"p":"She stared across the lake. “We could stay close.”"},{"p":"“We could. But the weather’s building, and I don’t need to take you two out to prove I can handle a boat.”"},{"p":"Hazel kicked at a loose board on the dock. I understood the feeling so well that I almost laughed."},{"p":"Pete carried the tackle box back toward the cabin. “We can fish from shore, or we can go into town. Your choice.”"},{"p":"Hazel waited until he was out of earshot."},{"p":"“I hate when he’s right.”"},{"p":"“Me too,” I said."},{"p":"“He’s not your dad.”"},{"p":"“No, but my flight instructor has the same talent.”"},{"p":"We sat with our feet above the water. Hazel picked at the label on her water bottle."},{"p":"“You really want to be a pilot?” she asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"The answer should have been easy. I could have told her about Uncle Everett and the fighter jets. About the first time I sat in the Cessna. About the way the ground looked when we climbed away from the runway."},{"p":"But those were pieces of an answer, and I wasn’t sure how to put them together."},{"p":"“I like how much there is to figure out,” I said eventually. “And when it all starts making sense, even for a few seconds, it’s…”"},{"p":"I searched for a word that didn’t sound ridiculous."},{"p":"“Good?” Hazel offered."},{"p":"“Really good.”"},{"p":"She nodded as if that was enough."},{"p":"“My thing is boats,” she said. “Except apparently today my thing is looking at boats.”"},{"p":"I pulled out my phone and opened the picture Imani had sent. The arrows on her diagram looked almost cheerful."},{"p":"“Can I show you something?”"},{"p":"Hazel leaned over. “Is it homework?”"},{"p":"“Sort of.”"},{"p":"“That’s a dangerous way to start a conversation.”"},{"p":"I pointed to the little airplane. “This arrow is where the airplane is pointed. This one is the wind. And this is the path it makes over the ground. They aren’t always the same.”"},{"p":"Hazel studied it. “Like if I paddle a canoe straight across a river and the current carries me downstream?”"},{"p":"“Yeah. Except an airplane moves through air instead of water.”"},{"p":"“So you point it upstream?”"},{"p":"“Sometimes you point into the wind to hold the ground track you want. It depends on the wind and what you’re trying to do.”"},{"p":"She took the phone and turned it sideways."},{"p":"“What if the wind changes halfway?”"},{"p":"“You adjust.”"},{"p":"“What if it changes a lot?”"},{"p":"“Then maybe you change the plan.”"},{"p":"Hazel handed the phone back. “That sounds like what Pete just did.”"},{"p":"I looked out over the water. The farther waves were breaking more often now. A small fishing boat was heading toward shore, bouncing enough that I could see its bow lift."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “I guess it does.”"},{"p":"We went into town instead. Hazel showed me a shop with an enormous wooden fish hanging above the door, and we spent twenty minutes arguing over which souvenir was the ugliest. She voted for a rubber duck wearing sunglasses. I voted for a refrigerator magnet shaped like a fish with human teeth."},{"p":"Dad bought the magnet."},{"p":"“Why?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“It’s educational,” he said."},{"p":"“About what?”"},{"p":"“Bad decisions.”"},{"p":"That afternoon, the wind grew stronger. Rain came in short bursts that rattled the cabin windows. We played cards at the kitchen table while Pete made popcorn and Dana tried to teach Dad a game whose rules changed every time he began winning."},{"p":"My phone buzzed with a message from Beckett."},{"p":"*You missed the world’s worst paper-airplane contest. Rafe’s plane went backward.*"},{"p":"A second message appeared."},{"p":"*He says that counts as a crosswind demonstration.*"},{"p":"I laughed and showed Hazel."},{"p":"“Your friends are weird,” she said."},{"p":"“They’re pretty great.”"},{"p":"It came out before I could make it sound less embarrassing."},{"p":"She didn’t tease me. She just dealt another card."},{"p":"That night, after the rain moved away, I went down to the dock. The boat was still tied securely where Pete had left it. Across the lake, a pale strip of light sat beneath the clouds."},{"p":"I’d traveled hundreds of miles, missed an academy session, and spent a whole day not going anywhere in a boat."},{"p":"It should have felt like a wasted day."},{"p":"Instead, I could remember Hazel’s face when she was angry, the sound of rain on the roof, the ridiculous fish magnet, and the way the waves looked different beyond the trees."},{"p":"I took out my notebook and drew the shoreline. Then I added a small arrow where the wind had come from in the morning and another where it had come from in the afternoon."},{"p":"Below them, I wrote: *What you see from the dock isn’t always what you’ll find on the lake.*"},{"p":"I stared at the sentence. It sounded a little too much like something Everett would say."},{"p":"I crossed it out and wrote: *Ask Pete before getting in the boat.*"},{"p":"That was better."}]},{"t":"Chapter Five — The Windsock’s Other End","blocks":[{"p":"By the time we got home from Michigan, the fish magnet had fallen off the refrigerator twice."},{"p":"Dad insisted the problem was our refrigerator. Mom insisted the problem was the magnet, which had a head roughly the size of a tennis ball and a magnetic strip the size of a fingernail. I suggested we stop trying to attach a fish with human teeth to an appliance."},{"p":"Nobody listened."},{"p":"On Saturday, the fish was lying in the fruit bowl. I took a picture and sent it to Hazel."},{"p":"*Your gift has escaped again.*"},{"p":"She replied with a photograph of Houghton Lake. The water was so smooth it looked like somebody had ironed it."},{"p":"*Of course it’s calm now,* she wrote."},{"p":"I stared at the picture longer than I meant to. In Michigan, I’d spent half a day wishing the wind would stop. Back in Virginia, I was about to spend half a day learning what to do when it didn’t."},{"p":"Dad drove me to Hampton Roads Executive Airport with the windows cracked because the air conditioner had started making a noise like a jar full of bees."},{"p":"“Maybe we should take it to somebody,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s the plan.”"},{"p":"“When?”"},{"p":"“When it starts making a noise like a jar full of angry bees.”"},{"p":"We pulled into the parking lot. Beyond the fence, the white Cessna sat outside the hangar with its wings level and its nose pointed toward the ramp. The sky was blue, but the windsock near the field wasn’t hanging straight down. It swung one way, paused, and twitched back."},{"p":"Dad noticed me looking."},{"p":"“What’s it saying?”"},{"p":"“That the wind’s moving.”"},{"p":"“Excellent. Fourteen years old and already a meteorologist.”"},{"p":"“I mean, it’s shifting.”"},{"p":"“Then tell Mara that. I’ll pick you up at noon.”"},{"p":"Inside the classroom, Rafe was trying to balance a pencil on the tip of his finger. Beckett was explaining why that was impossible using words that sounded suspiciously like a physics textbook. Imani was drawing a line across a sheet of graph paper."},{"p":"“You’re back,” she said without looking up."},{"p":"“I was only gone a week.”"},{"p":"“Beckett told us you got recruited by the Coast Guard.”"},{"p":"“Did not,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“You said he was learning water operations.”"},{"p":"“That could mean anything.”"},{"p":"Rafe’s pencil hit the floor."},{"p":"“I had it,” he announced."},{"p":"“You had gravity,” Imani said."},{"p":"Mara came through the door carrying a clipboard and a pair of battered foam airplanes. She stopped at the board, drew a runway shaped like a long rectangle, and put an arrow across it from left to right."},{"p":"“Who wants to tell me where the wind is coming from?”"},{"p":"“Left,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. And if we’re trying to keep an airplane aligned with this runway while flying the approach?”"},{"p":"“Point it into the wind a little,” I said. “Crab.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “That’s one way to correct for drift on approach. But what do we need as the wheels touch?”"},{"p":"Rafe lifted a hand. “Wheels going the same direction as the runway?”"},{"p":"“Exactly. We don’t want the airplane traveling sideways across the pavement. Today we’ll talk about why a crosswind landing involves more than pointing the nose at the runway.”"},{"p":"Beckett raised his hand. “Are we doing one?”"},{"p":"“Not until I know the conditions are appropriate, and not until we’re ready. You can learn the idea right here.”"},{"p":"She laid one foam airplane on the table. The other she held above the runway she’d drawn."},{"p":"“Imagine you’re looking down from above. The airplane’s nose points here. The wind pushes it this way. What happens to its track over the ground?”"},{"p":"“It drifts,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Unless?”"},{"p":"“We correct for it.”"},{"p":"Mara tilted the model slightly toward the wind. “Right. A crab angle helps maintain the intended ground track while airborne. But close to touchdown, we need the airplane’s longitudinal axis aligned with the runway, and we need to control the sideways drift. In many training airplanes, that means using a wing-low sideslip: aileron into the wind, opposite rudder as needed to keep the nose aligned.”"},{"p":"I pictured moving my hands one way and my feet another. My brain objected."},{"p":"“So you’re crossing the controls?” I asked."},{"p":"“Using them for different jobs. Aileron helps control drift. Rudder helps control where the nose points. We practice that carefully, with an instructor, and only within the airplane’s limitations and our own.”"},{"p":"Rafe held his hands up like a steering wheel. “I think I just sprained my imagination.”"},{"p":"“That’s why we have the foam airplanes,” Mara said."},{"p":"She had us take turns moving the models above the drawn runway. Beckett kept trying to explain the whole thing before anyone else could touch one. Imani drew little arrows to show which way the air was moving. Rafe gave his model a radio call and made it request permission to land at a runway that existed only in marker ink."},{"p":"When my turn came, I moved the airplane toward the runway, angled its nose into the imaginary wind, and then straightened it for touchdown."},{"p":"“Looks good,” I said."},{"p":"Mara moved her finger across the board. “But where is it going?”"},{"p":"I looked again. I’d straightened the nose without stopping the sideways drift. My airplane was about to land on the grass."},{"p":"“Oh.”"},{"p":"“That’s an important oh.”"},{"p":"I moved the wing down a little on the windward side, keeping the model lined up with the runway."},{"p":"“Better?”"},{"p":"“Better idea,” Mara said. “A model won’t teach you the feel of the controls, but it can help you understand what each one is for.”"},{"p":"Afterward, we went outside. Mara stopped us well away from the active ramp and pointed toward the windsock."},{"p":"“Lincoln, what direction is the wind coming from?”"},{"p":"I watched it swing."},{"p":"“Mostly from over there,” I said, pointing toward the sock’s narrow end."},{"p":"Mara shook her head. “Look at which end is attached to the pole.”"},{"p":"The broad opening faced into the wind. The narrow end streamed away from it."},{"p":"I felt my ears warm. I’d watched windsocks for more than a year and still managed to read one backward."},{"p":"“Right,” I said. “It’s coming from the open end.”"},{"p":"Beckett opened his mouth. I braced myself."},{"p":"Then he said, “I used to do that too.”"},{"p":"“Really?”"},{"p":"“Yeah. Until I built one and put it in front of a fan.”"},{"p":"“Of course you built one.”"},{"p":"“Of course.”"},{"p":"The windsock rose, drooped, and rose again. Whatever it was telling us kept changing."},{"p":"Mara took out her clipboard. “We’re not flying a crosswind exercise today. The gusts are higher than I’d like for this stage of training. We have other useful work to do.”"},{"p":"I heard the disappointment in the silence around me. Rafe kicked a pebble. Beckett looked at the Cessna."},{"p":"A year ago, I might have asked if we could go anyway. Today I had a different question."},{"p":"“Can we watch how the wind changes and compare it with the weather report?”"},{"p":"Mara smiled. “That’s exactly what I was hoping someone would suggest.”"},{"p":"Rafe groaned. “Great. Lincoln’s volunteering us for homework.”"},{"p":"“Outdoor homework,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s still homework.”"},{"p":"We spent twenty minutes making observations and comparing them with the airport’s reported conditions. Imani recorded the changes. Beckett argued about whether a gust counted if nobody was looking at the windsock when it happened. Rafe made up a game in which we guessed where it would point next."},{"p":"I got three guesses wrong in a row."},{"p":"The fourth time, I didn’t guess. I watched."},{"p":"That turned out to be more interesting."}]},{"t":"Chapter Six — The Empty Seat","blocks":[{"p":"On Tuesday evening, I was supposed to have a lesson with Mara."},{"p":"I knew because I’d written it on the kitchen calendar, in my phone, and in the notebook I kept in my backpack. I’d also reminded Mom twice, which she said was impressive for someone who still needed reminders to put his cereal bowl in the sink."},{"p":"Then Mara called."},{"p":"A line of showers had developed west of the airport. The weather no longer suited the lesson she’d planned, so she canceled before we drove out to wait."},{"p":"I sat at the kitchen table with my shoes on and my flight bag beside the chair."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said."},{"p":"“I’m sorry, Lincoln. We’ll reschedule.”"},{"p":"“Yeah. I understand.”"},{"p":"I did understand. That was the annoying part."},{"p":"When I hung up, Mom was chopping carrots at the counter."},{"p":"“No flying?”"},{"p":"“No flying.”"},{"p":"“You can take your shoes off.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"I didn’t take them off."},{"p":"She glanced at me but let it go. Through the window over the sink, I could see a bright patch of sky above the neighbor’s roof. It didn’t look like a canceled-flight sky. It looked like Tuesday."},{"p":"My phone buzzed. Rafe had sent a picture of the radar from a weather app with a row of rain showers moving across the region."},{"p":"*Your airplane is allergic to clouds,* he wrote."},{"p":"*Not all clouds,* I typed."},{"p":"*Congratulations. You passed my exam.*"},{"p":"I opened the image and enlarged it. The rain was still far from our house. But the airport wasn’t our house, and Mara’s planned route covered more than the square of sky outside our kitchen window."},{"p":"Dad came in carrying a bag of library books he’d borrowed on his day off. He looked at my shoes, then the flight bag."},{"p":"“Either you’re leaving or you’ve started a new fashion.”"},{"p":"“Canceled.”"},{"p":"“Ah.” He set the books down. “Want to help me fix the air conditioner?”"},{"p":"“Do you know how?”"},{"p":"“No. I was hoping you did.”"},{"p":"I laughed despite myself."},{"p":"We ended up at the dining table with the weather app open on my phone and a paper map from the academy spread between the salt shaker and a stack of unopened mail. Dad had taken out his glasses and was holding them by one arm."},{"p":"“Show me why she canceled,” he said."},{"p":"“Because the weather’s bad.”"},{"p":"“I got that part.”"},{"p":"I pointed at the line of showers. “This is moving toward the airport. And Mara said the conditions weren’t right for what we were supposed to practice.”"},{"p":"“What were you supposed to practice?”"},{"p":"“Wind corrections. Maybe some approaches, depending on how things looked.”"},{"p":"“So couldn’t you just go up before the rain arrives?”"},{"p":"I’d wanted to ask the same thing. Somehow it sounded different coming from Dad."},{"p":"“Maybe we’d get back in time,” I said. “But we might not. And there could be gusts ahead of the rain. Plus, we wouldn’t have much time to do the lesson properly.”"},{"p":"Dad nodded. “Then the cancellation makes sense.”"},{"p":"“It’s still disappointing.”"},{"p":"“Both things can be true.”"},{"p":"He said it casually, like he was talking about the fish magnet. I looked down at the map. The airfield was a tiny mark surrounded by roads, towns, rivers, and all the places I couldn’t see from the kitchen."},{"p":"My phone rang again. This time it was Uncle Everett."},{"p":"“Your mother tells me the weather won,” he said."},{"p":"“Apparently.”"},{"p":"“Did you look at the forecast?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Good. What changed?”"},{"p":"I told him about the showers and the gusts, and how I hadn’t realized the wind could be rough ahead of a line of rain."},{"p":"“Out of curiosity,” I said, “did you ever get really annoyed when a flight was canceled?”"},{"p":"Everett laughed. “Of course.”"},{"p":"“I thought you’d say no.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because you’re always the one telling me safety comes first.”"},{"p":"“Safety coming first doesn’t mean you stop wanting to fly.”"},{"p":"I heard something clink on his end of the phone. He was probably working on one of his old radios. I pictured him sitting at the bench in his garage, squinting at a tiny screw he’d dropped for the third time."},{"p":"“Sometimes,” he said, “the hard part is accepting that the right decision won’t feel like a reward.”"},{"p":"I considered that. Then I looked at the clock."},{"p":"“I was supposed to be in the airplane by now.”"},{"p":"“Then you’re allowed to be grumpy for a while. Just don’t make everybody else pay for it.”"},{"p":"“That sounds suspiciously specific.”"},{"p":"“I was fourteen once.”"},{"p":"“Was there even weather back then?”"},{"p":"He laughed hard enough that I had to pull the phone away from my ear."},{"p":"After dinner, I went out onto the porch. The first rain had arrived, not a thunderstorm, just heavy drops that struck the steps and made dark circles on the concrete. Wind shoved the wet leaves along the driveway."},{"p":"I took a photograph and sent it to our group chat."},{"p":"*Turns out Mara was right.*"},{"p":"Beckett replied immediately."},{"p":"*Print that. Frame it. Put it in the hangar.*"},{"p":"Imani sent a cloud emoji, followed by: *What did you learn?*"},{"p":"I could have answered with something about forecasts and gust fronts. Those were real things, and I wanted to understand them better. But my clearest lesson wasn’t on the chart."},{"p":"*That I can be mad about a canceled flight and still know it was the right call,* I wrote."},{"p":"For once, nobody made a joke."},{"p":"Then Rafe sent a picture of a fish magnet with human teeth."},{"p":"*Also this thing is horrifying. Why does your dad own it?*"},{"p":"I looked through the window. The fish had been returned to the refrigerator with two extra magnets holding it up, one on either side of its ridiculous face."},{"p":"*Structural reinforcement,* I typed."},{"p":"Outside, the rain kept coming. My flight bag was still by the door. I would need it again, just not tonight."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seven — The Road Beneath the Wing","blocks":[{"p":"The following Tuesday morning, Mom made me take the trash out before we left for the airport."},{"p":"I had my flight bag over one shoulder, my shoes half tied, and a banana in my mouth. The trash bag had leaked something that smelled like old pickles onto the kitchen floor."},{"p":"“Careful,” Mom said."},{"p":"I looked down at the yellowish puddle. “You said that too late.”"},{"p":"“Then consider it advice for next time.”"},{"p":"After cleaning the floor, changing my socks, and rescuing my banana from the counter, I was seven minutes behind schedule. I spent the whole drive looking at the clock on the dashboard."},{"p":"“Are you going to stare the minutes backward?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“I might.”"},{"p":"“Let me know if it works. I could use an extra hour on Thursdays.”"},{"p":"At Hampton Roads Executive Airport, Mara Ellery was standing beside the Cessna with a clipboard tucked under one arm. Three strands had escaped her dark-blond ponytail. The windsock beyond the hangars hung at an angle, occasionally twitching."},{"p":"“You made it,” she said."},{"p":"“Barely.”"},{"p":"“We haven’t started the engine yet, so I think we’re all right.”"},{"p":"I expected her to ask whether I’d reviewed the lesson. Instead, she pointed toward the windsock."},{"p":"“What’s it telling you?”"},{"p":"“Wind’s coming from that direction.” I pointed. “Not very strong.”"},{"p":"“Good. And what isn’t it telling you?”"},{"p":"I thought about the flag at the baseball field and the rough water on Houghton Lake."},{"p":"“What it’ll be doing ten minutes from now?”"},{"p":"“That’s one answer.”"},{"p":"I looked toward the trees beyond the runway. Their tops moved in little waves. “Or what the wind’s doing farther up.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “That’s the one I wanted you to notice.”"},{"p":"We went through the preflight inspection together. She let me lead, but she didn’t let me hurry. When I reached the left wing, I stopped to check a hinge and asked her to look at something that seemed unfamiliar. Mara showed me why it was normal for this airplane. I felt stupid for two seconds, then remembered last summer’s red tag."},{"p":"“Better to ask,” I said."},{"p":"“I was going to say that.”"},{"p":"“I’m saving you time.”"},{"p":"“That’s thoughtful of you.”"},{"p":"Once we were seated and buckled in, Mara took care of the parts of the departure that required her experience and judgment. I followed the checklist with her, trying not to race ahead. The engine’s vibration came up through the soles of my shoes. Through the side window, I watched a small patch of grass flatten and lift again in the breeze."},{"p":"We were heading to a nearby practice area for a dual lesson. Mara handled the radio and the decisions about where we could safely fly. My job was to look outside, fly when she transferred control, and learn to hold a ground track against the wind."},{"p":"After takeoff, the houses below shrank into tidy squares. A road ran between two patches of trees, bent around a pond, and straightened toward a cluster of buildings. From the ground, I probably would have called it a boring road. From up here, it looked like a line someone had drawn with a ruler and then changed their mind about."},{"p":"Mara leveled the airplane and completed her checks."},{"p":"“You have the flight controls.”"},{"p":"“I have the flight controls.”"},{"p":"The yoke felt familiar, which was both comforting and dangerous. Familiarity made me want to skip straight to being good at this."},{"p":"“See that road?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“The one by the pond?”"},{"p":"“Yes. For this exercise, we’ll use it as a visual reference. Keep us tracking along it while maintaining our assigned altitude. I’ll keep an eye on the bigger picture.”"},{"p":"I lined the nose up with the road."},{"p":"For a little while, it worked."},{"p":"Then the road began sliding sideways beneath us."},{"p":"I eased into a shallow bank toward it. The road crept back toward the center of the windshield. I straightened the wings."},{"p":"The road drifted away again."},{"p":"“It’s doing it on purpose,” I said."},{"p":"“Roads are notorious for that.”"},{"p":"I tried another correction, a little bigger this time. The nose swung across the road, and now the pond was approaching from the wrong side."},{"p":"“Look farther ahead,” Mara said. “Not just at what’s directly below the nose.”"},{"p":"I picked a point where the road disappeared into the trees. That helped me see the movement earlier. I eased the airplane back toward the line."},{"p":"“Better. What are you correcting for?”"},{"p":"“Wind.”"},{"p":"“What is the wind changing?”"},{"p":"I almost said our heading. Then I remembered the arrows Imani had drawn in class."},{"p":"“Our track across the ground.”"},{"p":"“Right. The airplane can point one direction while moving across the ground in another. You don’t have to aim its nose straight down the road to travel along the road.”"},{"p":"I looked at the compass, then out at the distant trees. The nose was angled slightly toward the side the wind was coming from. It felt wrong, like walking down a hallway while looking at the wall."},{"p":"But the road stayed where I wanted it."},{"p":"For six or seven seconds."},{"p":"Then I stared too long at the road and let the airplane climb."},{"p":"“Altitude,” Mara reminded me."},{"p":"I checked the altimeter, lowered the nose too abruptly, and felt my stomach lift."},{"p":"“Small change,” she said. “And then give it a chance to respond.”"},{"p":"I eased off. The airplane settled."},{"p":"This was the part nobody put on posters. There was no heroic music. No dazzling view that made everything easy. There was just me in a headset, trying to do two things while forgetting four others."},{"p":"We repeated the exercise with another road, then a stretch of shoreline that curved enough to make the whole thing more confusing. Mara showed me how to choose a reference point well ahead rather than chase every bend. She also made it clear that the exercise wasn’t a substitute for navigation or proper traffic scanning. I had to keep looking for other airplanes, checking the instruments, and listening when she spoke."},{"p":"At one point I held the track reasonably well for long enough to start congratulating myself."},{"p":"Then Mara said, “Where’s your altitude?”"},{"p":"I checked. “Not where I left it.”"},{"p":"“No, it wandered off while you were admiring your work.”"},{"p":"“Can I get it back?”"},{"p":"“I believe that’s the idea.”"},{"p":"We traded control a few times so I could watch her technique. When she made a correction, I barely saw her hands move. The horizon shifted gently instead of swinging across the windshield."},{"p":"“Are you actually moving the yoke?” I asked."},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“That’s irritating.”"},{"p":"“I’ll try to make it more dramatic next time.”"},{"p":"On the return, she handled the arrival and landing. I watched the runway appear ahead, then slide a little to one side as she adjusted the airplane’s path. She explained that the wind correction used during our ground-track exercise was related to, but not the same as, the precise control required during an approach and landing. Those were skills she’d teach in stages. I wasn’t ready to handle them myself."},{"p":"The wheels touched with a short chirp. I felt my shoulders drop."},{"p":"After shutdown, Mara opened her clipboard."},{"p":"“Tell me what went well.”"},{"p":"“I didn’t fly us into the pond.”"},{"p":"“A useful accomplishment. Try again.”"},{"p":"I thought about it. “I noticed the drift sooner when I looked farther ahead. And I didn’t keep fighting the yoke as much.”"},{"p":"“What needs work?”"},{"p":"“Everything else.”"},{"p":"She raised an eyebrow."},{"p":"“Fine. Altitude. Scanning. Remembering that the road doesn’t have to be right under the nose.”"},{"p":"“That’s a workable list.”"},{"p":"She wrote something down, then showed me the page. Three short notes. No dramatic grade. No gold star. Just a record of what we’d practiced and what we’d do next."},{"p":"“Does the wind always make it this hard?” I asked."},{"p":"“Some days the air is smoother. Some days it isn’t. The trick is to recognize the conditions, know what you’re capable of, and work within the limits of the airplane and the pilot. That’s why I’m sitting here with you.”"},{"p":"I looked toward the runway. The windsock had lifted a little higher than it had that morning."},{"p":"“What if I had lined up the nose with the road and never corrected?”"},{"p":"“We’d have ended up beside it, not over it.”"},{"p":"I imagined trying to explain to someone that I’d been pointing at the right place the whole time while flying somewhere else."},{"p":"That sounded exactly like something I would have done last summer."},{"p":"On the drive home, I took out my notebook and drew a road, an airplane, and two arrows. One arrow showed where the nose pointed. The other showed the path over the ground. My drawing looked like a bird had landed on a paper clip."},{"p":"Mom glanced over when we stopped at a red light."},{"p":"“Successful flight?”"},{"p":"“I got lost on a straight road.”"},{"p":"“In the air?”"},{"p":"“Technically, no. I could see the road the whole time.”"},{"p":"“Then that’s a very specific kind of lost.”"},{"p":"I drew a third arrow for the wind."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “It is.”"}]},{"t":"Chapter Eight — The Argument in the Hangar","blocks":[{"p":"By Saturday, Beckett had built a wind tunnel."},{"p":"That was his name for a cardboard box with a desk fan taped to one end and enough holes in the sides to suggest a fork attack."},{"p":"“It’s not a wind tunnel,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s a tunnel. There’s wind.”"},{"p":"“There’s also a loose piece of tape getting sucked into the fan.”"},{"p":"Beckett pressed it down. “Improvement noted.”"},{"p":"Imani was at the worktable with a notebook and a bag of paper strips. Rafe had found a rolling office chair and was testing whether he could cross the room without using his feet."},{"p":"“Don’t hit the toolbox,” I told him."},{"p":"“That’s a terrible lack of confidence.”"},{"p":"He hit the toolbox."},{"p":"The clang brought Mara out of the adjoining room. She took in the chair, the fan, and the expression on Rafe’s face."},{"p":"“Do I need to ask?”"},{"p":"“No,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Excellent. Wheels on the floor, please.”"},{"p":"The academy had set aside the morning for a ground workshop. Mara wanted us to compare how small model airplanes behaved in moving air. Nobody was going to fly a real airplane that day. The forecast included gusts that were beyond the conditions she’d chosen for our current lessons, and she had other students to supervise later."},{"p":"Beckett had arrived with three foam gliders, two paper airplanes, and a wooden ruler marked in pencil."},{"p":"“I’ve been testing different tail sizes,” he announced."},{"p":"“Did you write down what you changed?” Imani asked."},{"p":"“Mostly.”"},{"p":"“Where?”"},{"p":"Beckett tapped his forehead."},{"p":"Imani stared at him."},{"p":"“Fine,” he said. “Somewhere less likely to survive a bicycle accident.”"},{"p":"Mara put us in charge of a simple comparison. We were to send identical paper gliders across a marked section of the room, first with the fan off and then with a gentle cross-breeze. We had to keep the launch point and method as consistent as possible and record where the gliders landed. The fan couldn’t reproduce flight conditions, but it let us observe one effect on the models."},{"p":"We made four gliders from the same kind of paper. Beckett folded each one with a precision I normally reserved for hiding a bad report card. Imani labeled the undersides A through D. Rafe drew tiny faces on the noses."},{"p":"“Now they have personalities,” he said."},{"p":"“Now they’re less aerodynamic,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Prove it.”"},{"p":"The first few launches went badly. One glider dived into a chair. Another sailed straight across the room and disappeared behind a cabinet. Rafe retrieved it with a broom, which caused a rain of dust and one ancient rubber band to fall on his head."},{"p":"“Field conditions,” he said, coughing."},{"p":"When we turned on the fan, the gliders landed farther to one side. Not always by the same amount. One bad throw could hide the effect we were trying to measure."},{"p":"“That’s the problem,” Imani said. “We keep changing the launch.”"},{"p":"“We’re people,” I said. “It’s difficult not to.”"},{"p":"“We could build a launcher,” Beckett offered."},{"p":"Mara, who was checking a stack of lesson sheets, looked up. “Not with anything that stores much energy, you couldn’t.”"},{"p":"“I was thinking of a folded-cardboard guide.”"},{"p":"“That we can discuss.”"},{"p":"Beckett took two strips of cardboard and began building a narrow channel to help us aim the gliders consistently. I watched him fold one edge and reach for tape."},{"p":"“You need to make it wider,” I said."},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because the wings will hit.”"},{"p":"“The wings go above the guide.”"},{"p":"“Not if you tilt it like that.”"},{"p":"“I’m not going to tilt it like that.”"},{"p":"“You always tilt things like that.”"},{"p":"Beckett put down the tape."},{"p":"“What’s that supposed to mean?”"},{"p":"That came out sharper than I meant. Worse, I still wanted to prove I was right."},{"p":"“Last summer you built that glider with the paper clip on the nose, and it kept diving. Then you said it was working because it crashed the same way every time.”"},{"p":"“It was a joke.”"},{"p":"“I know. I’m saying—”"},{"p":"“You’re saying I don’t know what I’m doing.”"},{"p":"“No, I’m saying the guide is crooked.”"},{"p":"“Then make your own.”"},{"p":"He pushed the cardboard toward me. The tape dispenser slid off the table and hit the floor."},{"p":"Rafe stopped smiling. Imani set her pencil down."},{"p":"Mara came over, picked up the tape dispenser, and put it back between us."},{"p":"“What’s the problem?” she asked."},{"p":"“Lincoln thinks I can’t build a straight guide,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I didn’t say that.”"},{"p":"“You basically did.”"},{"p":"Mara looked at me. “Did you mean to?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then tell him what you meant, without bringing up last year’s paper clip.”"},{"p":"I felt my ears get warm. “I thought the wings would catch if the guide tilted. I should’ve said that instead of making it about you.”"},{"p":"Beckett rubbed a thumb over the edge of the cardboard."},{"p":"“It’s not tilted,” he said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"He looked up. “You don’t know. You haven’t tested it.”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth, then shut it."},{"p":"The worst part was that he was right."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “Let’s test it.”"},{"p":"We set the guide on the table, lined up a glider, and tried a launch. Its left wing caught the cardboard and flipped it sideways into Rafe’s stomach."},{"p":"Rafe doubled over laughing. “I have been attacked by science.”"},{"p":"Beckett examined the guide. “It needs to be wider.”"},{"p":"I said nothing."},{"p":"“Don’t,” he warned."},{"p":"“I didn’t.”"},{"p":"“You were thinking it.”"},{"p":"“I can’t help what happens inside my head.”"},{"p":"Imani slid her notebook between us. “Can you two help what happens outside your heads? We have seven minutes before Mara wants our results.”"},{"p":"We widened the guide and tried again. The launch was cleaner. We kept the fan at the same setting and marked each landing with masking tape. The results were still messy, but a pattern began to show: the cross-breeze shifted the landing spots, even though the gliders started in nearly the same direction."},{"p":"Imani drew a simple chart. Beckett checked the distances. Rafe wrote a heading at the top: THINGS THE AIR DID WITHOUT ASKING."},{"p":"“Not exactly scientific,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Scientifically accurate,” Rafe argued."},{"p":"When Mara returned, we explained our method and showed her the tape marks on the floor. She asked what we could conclude."},{"p":"“The breeze pushed them sideways,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. What can’t you conclude?”"},{"p":"“That a paper airplane works exactly like a Cessna,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Or that the fan is the same as the wind outside,” Beckett added."},{"p":"“Or that Rafe should ever be allowed to label an experiment,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s discrimination against creativity,” Rafe said."},{"p":"Mara smiled. “All fair points. Now connect it to what we discussed about ground track.”"},{"p":"I told them about the road beneath the airplane during my lesson. I used two pencils on the table to show how I had aimed the nose one way while the airplane moved along the road in another direction. Beckett asked whether the wind had been steady. I said it hadn’t felt steady to me, though I couldn’t tell how much was wind and how much was my own uneven flying."},{"p":"“That’s why you make small corrections and check what happens,” he said."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"He looked pleased for half a second, then tried to hide it."},{"p":"After the workshop, I found him outside the hangar collecting scraps of cardboard that had blown across the pavement. The afternoon sun had turned the ramp almost white. Farther down, a mechanic rolled a tool cart through an open hangar door."},{"p":"I picked up a strip of cardboard and held it out."},{"p":"“Hey,” I said. “Sorry about earlier.”"},{"p":"Beckett took it. “You already apologized.”"},{"p":"“I know. It was a bad apology.”"},{"p":"“It was okay.”"},{"p":"“I was trying to prove I knew more than you.”"},{"p":"“You do that a lot.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"He shoved the scraps into the box. “Sometimes I do it too.”"},{"p":"That was about as close as Beckett came to giving a speech about feelings. I appreciated it."},{"p":"He nodded toward the runway. “How was your flight?”"},{"p":"“Harder than it sounded in class.”"},{"p":"“Everything’s harder than it sounds in class.”"},{"p":"“You haven’t tried my dad’s homemade bookshelf instructions.”"},{"p":"“Did it fall over?”"},{"p":"“Not yet. We don’t put anything heavy on the left side.”"},{"p":"Beckett laughed. Then he pulled a small foam glider from his bag. Its nose was held together with tape, and one wing had a pencil mark near the tip."},{"p":"“I’m working on a better version,” he said. “Want to try it?”"},{"p":"“Does it have a paper clip?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“That’s disappointing.”"},{"p":"We walked to the grassy area Mara had approved for the workshop. Beckett tossed the glider into the breeze. It climbed a little, banked left, and settled into the grass."},{"p":"“Not bad,” I said."},{"p":"“Needs work.”"},{"p":"I picked it up and handed it back."},{"p":"“Don’t we all?”"},{"p":"He gave me a look. “Please don’t start talking like your uncle.”"},{"p":"“Fair.”"},{"p":"My phone buzzed. It was a message from Hazel Mercer in Michigan, with a photograph of Houghton Lake under a bright sky. The water looked smooth near shore, but farther out a line of darker ripples crossed it at an angle."},{"p":"*Guess what the wind is doing today,* she’d written."},{"p":"I showed Beckett the picture."},{"p":"“Your lake friend?” he asked."},{"p":"“Hazel.”"},{"p":"He studied the water. “Wind’s stronger out there?”"},{"p":"“Maybe. Or it’s coming across a longer stretch of water.”"},{"p":"“Ask her.”"},{"p":"I typed: *Looks different farther out. What’s it actually like?*"},{"p":"I waited for the reply instead of pretending I already knew."},{"p":"It seemed like a small thing. Probably nobody else would have noticed."},{"p":"But for once, I was curious about the answer more than I was worried about sounding smart."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nine — The Line on the Water","blocks":[{"p":"Hazel’s answer arrived while Beckett was trying to make his foam glider fly backward."},{"p":"*Wind picked up after lunch. Grandma made us bring the canoe in. Also that dark patch is mostly clouds making shadows. Nice guess, though.*"},{"p":"I read it twice."},{"p":"“What’s funny?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“Nothing.”"},{"p":"“You made the face you make when somebody beats you at something.”"},{"p":"“I have a face for that?”"},{"p":"“You have several.”"},{"p":"I showed him the message. He took one look at the picture, then at the sky over our own runway."},{"p":"“Could be both,” he said. “Wind and shadows.”"},{"p":"“That’s what I was going to say.”"},{"p":"“Of course it was.”"},{"p":"On Monday, the photograph came up again, this time on the kitchen table. Dad borrowed my phone after listening to me explain lake wind through most of dinner. He set it between the salt shaker and a bowl of green beans."},{"p":"“Can you tell which way the wind is blowing?” he asked."},{"p":"“Not from one picture. Hazel said it changed after lunch.”"},{"p":"“Can you tell how strong it is?”"},{"p":"“Not exactly.”"},{"p":"Dad adjusted his glasses. “You’re getting harder to trick.”"},{"p":"“It’s exhausting for everyone,” Mom said."},{"p":"Work papers sat beside her plate. She wore the look she got after a full day of solving other people’s problems. Dad was planning a summer reading event at the library. I was planning to survive Tuesday’s lesson without confusing the rudder pedals with a footrest."},{"p":"Mom pushed her papers aside. “Speaking of Tuesday. I can drive you there, but I can’t stay past four. I’ve got the records meeting.”"},{"p":"“I can pick him up,” Dad said. “Unless the library event runs long.”"},{"p":"“That’s a lot of unless,” I said."},{"p":"“Welcome to having a family,” Mom said. “We’re a complicated machine.”"},{"p":"“Dad’s the part that makes strange noises.”"},{"p":"Dad looked offended. “Those are diagnostic noises.”"},{"p":"The next afternoon, Mara met me in the briefing room with a weather printout, a pencil, and a cup of coffee that smelled strong enough to remove paint."},{"p":"“We’re not flying yet,” she said when I reached for my bag."},{"p":"I sat down. “Is something wrong?”"},{"p":"“No. I want to see what you notice before we go outside.”"},{"p":"The sheet showed an aviation weather report from a nearby station and a simple runway diagram. Mara pointed to the wind information."},{"p":"“Tell me what this means.”"},{"p":"I read the numbers carefully. “Wind from two-four-zero degrees at nine knots, gusting to sixteen.”"},{"p":"“Good. What’s a knot?”"},{"p":"“A nautical mile per hour.”"},{"p":"“And the gust?”"},{"p":"“The wind isn’t staying at nine. It’s getting stronger for short periods.”"},{"p":"“Right. Now here’s our runway direction for this exercise. Is that wind straight down it?”"},{"p":"I compared the arrows. “No. Part of it would be across the runway.”"},{"p":"“How much?”"},{"p":"I started to calculate, then stopped. We’d done a simplified version in class, but I couldn’t remember the numbers well enough to pretend."},{"p":"“I need the chart,” I admitted."},{"p":"Mara slid a small crosswind-component chart across the table. I found the angle between the runway and wind direction, then followed the lines with my finger."},{"p":"“The crosswind part gets bigger with the gust,” I said."},{"p":"“Yes. The published wind is one piece of information. What else would you want to know?”"},{"p":"“Which runway we’d use. What’s happening at the airport itself. Whether the wind keeps changing.”"},{"p":"“Anything about us?”"},{"p":"I looked up. “You mean the airplane?”"},{"p":"“I mean us. The airplane, the pilot, the lesson, and the conditions we’re comfortable training in today.”"},{"p":"I stared at the chart. I’d spent so much time trying to learn what a crosswind was that I’d forgotten it wasn’t a puzzle with one correct number at the bottom."},{"p":"“Would you fly?” I asked."},{"p":"“I haven’t decided. That’s why we’re checking.”"},{"p":"Outside, the windsock pointed nearly straight for a moment, then softened and sagged. A small airplane taxied past with its nose wheel bouncing lightly over a seam in the pavement. Beyond the hangars, clouds moved across the sun, laying dark bands over the grass."},{"p":"It reminded me of Hazel’s lake photograph. I could see what the wind was doing in one place, right now. I couldn’t see the whole afternoon."},{"p":"Mara had me read the latest available weather information again. We talked through the difference between the direction wind came *from* and the direction an airplane traveled *toward*. I mixed them up once, which made Mara turn her pencil around on the table to demonstrate."},{"p":"“I thought I understood this,” I said."},{"p":"“You do understand part of it. That’s why we practice the rest.”"},{"p":"She looked outside again, then checked an updated report. The reported gusts had increased, and the wind direction was shifting. She capped her pen."},{"p":"“Not the conditions I want for your lesson today.”"},{"p":"My stomach sank anyway."},{"p":"“We can do some ground work,” she said. “Or I can send you home early.”"},{"p":"I thought of Mom’s meeting, Dad’s library event, and the way everybody had moved their afternoon around my flying. For a second I wanted to argue that the gusts might calm down."},{"p":"Instead, I asked, “Can we practice the runway diagram some more?”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “We can.”"},{"p":"I sent Mom a message to say we weren’t flying. She replied with a thumbs-up and *Still need pickup?*"},{"p":"*Yes. I’m learning on paper instead.*"},{"p":"*Paper is cheaper than airplane parts,* she wrote."},{"p":"I showed Mara. She laughed so hard she had to set down her coffee."},{"p":"By the time Dad arrived, I’d filled two pages with runway arrows, wind arrows, and triangles that stopped making sense whenever I looked away. I tucked the pages into my bag anyway."},{"p":"On the drive home, Dad asked if I’d been disappointed."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Did you learn anything?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"He waited for more. I watched a flag outside a car dealership snap straight, then droop as we stopped at the light."},{"p":"“It’s annoying,” I said, “that both things can be true.”"},{"p":"Dad smiled. “You should put that on a T-shirt.”"},{"p":"“Would you buy one?”"},{"p":"“Only if it came in librarian beige.”"},{"p":"I checked my phone again. Hazel had sent a second photograph: the canoe upside down on the shore, with a bright orange life jacket draped over it."},{"p":"*We got ice cream instead,* she’d written. *Wind didn’t ruin that.*"},{"p":"I sent back a picture of my messy diagrams."},{"p":"*Same. Except my ice cream is math.*"},{"p":"She replied almost immediately."},{"p":"*That sounds terrible.*"},{"p":"She wasn’t wrong."}]},{"t":"Chapter Ten — A Runway Isn’t a Target","blocks":[{"p":"Rafe showed up to Saturday’s academy session carrying a bright orange traffic cone."},{"p":"“Why?” I asked."},{"p":"“Because Mara said to bring something we could use as a marker.”"},{"p":"“Most people bring tape.”"},{"p":"“Most people lack vision.”"},{"p":"He set the cone on the classroom floor with the solemn care of someone placing a trophy on a shelf. Beckett had brought a ruler and a roll of string. Imani had brought her notebook. I’d brought the wrong lunch, which I discovered when I opened my bag and found a container of leftover broccoli instead of my sandwich."},{"p":"“I can trade you,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“For what?”"},{"p":"“Half a granola bar and a very good story.”"},{"p":"“No deal.”"},{"p":"Mara came in carrying a cardboard airplane cutout and a large sheet of paper. She stopped when she saw the cone."},{"p":"“Is that yours?”"},{"p":"“Educational equipment,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Then educate us by moving it out of the doorway.”"},{"p":"We pushed two tables together. Mara drew a runway down the center of the paper and set the cutout at one end. Then she drew an arrow across the runway."},{"p":"“Suppose there’s a crosswind from the right,” she said. “What will the airplane try to do if the pilot doesn’t correct for it?”"},{"p":"“Drift left,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Right. Now imagine we’re approaching to land. What do we need to manage?”"},{"p":"“Where the airplane goes,” I said."},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"Beckett rotated the cardboard airplane so its nose lined up with the runway. “Which way it’s pointing.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Those aren’t always the same thing. In a crosswind, we have to manage both the airplane’s path and its alignment. There are different techniques, and pilots transition between them as needed. Today we’re going to understand the problem, not practice landings.”"},{"p":"That last part mattered. We were fourteen, not airline captains. Moving cardboard across a table didn’t qualify us to practice crosswind landings."},{"p":"She showed us how an airplane could fly with its nose angled into the wind to maintain a desired track. Then she turned the cutout to line its nose with the runway and demonstrated, using the model, the different jobs the ailerons and rudder would have during a sideslip for a crosswind landing."},{"p":"I tried to copy her movements with my hands. One hand tilted the cardboard wings while the other nudged the tail. Somehow I made the model point toward Rafe’s cone."},{"p":"“Congratulations,” Rafe said. “You’ve landed on the parking lot.”"},{"p":"“I’m opening a new terminal.”"},{"p":"Imani took the cutout and tried. She kept it centered over the paper runway but forgot to straighten the nose."},{"p":"“That’s the part I keep losing,” she said. “I can picture one correction or the other, but not both at once.”"},{"p":"Mara didn’t immediately rescue us. She let us move the model back to the beginning and talk through what we were trying to accomplish."},{"p":"“It’s like two separate questions,” Beckett said. “Am I drifting off the line? And am I pointing down the line?”"},{"p":"“That’s a useful way to think about it,” Mara said. “Real airplanes also have changing winds, inertia, and a lot more going on than a cardboard model. The controls work together, and practice happens with an instructor in the airplane.”"},{"p":"We spent the next twenty minutes trying to move the cutout down the paper runway while Rafe supplied increasingly ridiculous weather reports."},{"p":"“Wind from the right at eleven knots,” he announced. “Light rain. One suspicious seagull.”"},{"p":"“Seagulls aren’t weather,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“You haven’t met this one.”"},{"p":"“Stop adding seagulls,” Imani told him. “I’m concentrating.”"},{"p":"On my third try, I moved the airplane too far left. I started over before anyone said anything. On my fourth, I got the track right but left the nose crooked. By the fifth, I was doing both parts slowly enough to understand them."},{"p":"“Better,” Mara said. “What changed?”"},{"p":"“I stopped trying to get to the end of the runway as fast as possible.”"},{"p":"“That will help in more than one kind of flying.”"},{"p":"After class, we went outside to watch operations from the approved viewing area. The wind had eased since Tuesday, but it still moved the grass in long patches. Mara pointed out how an airplane on final approach appeared to have its nose slightly angled relative to the runway as it corrected for drift. From where we stood, we couldn’t tell exactly which control inputs the pilot was making. Mara said so."},{"p":"Rafe leaned against the fence. “I thought flying was mostly pulling back and going up.”"},{"p":"“You’ve been here two summers,” I said."},{"p":"“And I’ve learned it’s also paperwork.”"},{"p":"“Checklists,” Imani corrected."},{"p":"“Paperwork with better consequences.”"},{"p":"Beckett was quiet. He had his arms folded over the fence rail and was watching the next airplane taxi toward the departure end."},{"p":"“You okay?” I asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.” He paused. “My dad might have to work next Saturday. I don’t know if I can get a ride.”"},{"p":"“Can’t somebody else take you?”"},{"p":"He gave me a look that said I had managed to ask a question without thinking about it."},{"p":"“Maybe,” he said. “We’re figuring it out.”"},{"p":"I thought about Mom and Dad rearranging their Tuesday for me. I’d complained about a leaky trash bag and seven lost minutes. Beckett wasn’t even sure he had a ride."},{"p":"“My dad’s driving me next Saturday,” I said. “We could ask if he can pick you up. If that’s okay with your family.”"},{"p":"Beckett studied the gravel beneath his shoes. “You don’t have to.”"},{"p":"“I know. I can still ask.”"},{"p":"He nodded once. “Thanks.”"},{"p":"Rafe, who had apparently decided the conversation needed rescuing, pointed at the orange cone sticking out of his backpack."},{"p":"“Also, I can offer you a ride,” he said. “But you’ll have to sit behind the cone.”"},{"p":"“Does the cone wear a seat belt?” Imani asked."},{"p":"“Of course. I’m not irresponsible.”"},{"p":"For the first time all morning, Beckett laughed without trying to hide it."},{"p":"That night, Dad agreed to ask Beckett’s parents about the ride. I wrote the next lesson on the family calendar, then looked at the runway diagram still poking out of my flight bag."},{"p":"All week I’d treated crosswinds as a problem you solved by pointing the airplane the right way."},{"p":"But the more I learned, the less that sounded true. You had to know where you were going, pay attention to what was pushing you off course, and make corrections that actually helped."},{"p":"I wasn’t especially good at any of those things yet."},{"p":"At least I was getting better at noticing."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eleven — The Passenger-Side Window","blocks":[{"p":"Saturday morning, Dad stood at the kitchen counter holding a travel mug in one hand and a note in the other."},{"p":"“Remind me,” he said. “Beckett’s house is the one with the blue shutters?”"},{"p":"“Gray shutters. Blue mailbox.”"},{"p":"“Right. Your mother says if I get lost, I’m not allowed to blame the GPS.”"},{"p":"Mom, who was packing her lunch for an extra shift at the records office, didn’t look up. “That’s because you blamed the GPS when you drove to the wrong library last month.”"},{"p":"“They had the same name.”"},{"p":"“One was a middle school.”"},{"p":"“They had books.”"},{"p":"I grabbed my flight bag before they could argue again about whether Dad needed medical treatment for his sense of direction."},{"p":"Outside, the morning was already warm. The sky had a thin, milky look, and the tops of the neighbor’s pine trees moved even though the air at driveway level felt almost still. Dad had said the wind was supposed to pick up later. I checked the forecast on my phone for the fourth time."},{"p":"“Seat belt,” he said as I climbed in."},{"p":"“I’m fourteen.”"},{"p":"“And still made of bones.”"},{"p":"Beckett was waiting on his front steps with his backpack at his feet. He had on the same academy T-shirt he’d worn last Saturday, but it was clean, and he’d tucked a notebook into the outside pocket of his bag. His father came out behind him, wiping his hands on a rag."},{"p":"“Morning,” Mr. Shaw called. “Thanks for doing this.”"},{"p":"“No trouble,” Dad said. “We’re heading that way.”"},{"p":"Mr. Shaw nodded, but he looked tired. He had a dark smear across one forearm and the kind of expression Dad got when a computer at the library refused to cooperate."},{"p":"“Text when you get there,” he told Beckett."},{"p":"“I will.”"},{"p":"“And no leaving your bag in their car.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"Beckett slid into the back seat beside me. For the first five minutes he studied the passing houses as if he had been assigned to count them."},{"p":"Dad offered to turn on the radio. We both said no at once."},{"p":"“Excellent,” Dad said. “A silent ride with two teenagers. Living the dream.”"},{"p":"Beckett’s mouth twitched."},{"p":"At a red light, Dad pointed toward a flag outside a hardware store. It stood nearly straight out from its pole."},{"p":"“Looks windy up there.”"},{"p":"“That doesn’t tell us how strong the wind is,” I said automatically."},{"p":"“I didn’t say it was.”"},{"p":"“Flags don’t tell you an exact speed, and buildings can change the airflow around them.”"},{"p":"Dad looked at me in the rearview mirror. “I was mostly making conversation.”"},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"Beckett turned toward his window. “You do that a lot.”"},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“Answer a question nobody asked.”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth, then shut it again. It was irritating when he was right because he usually looked pleased about it."},{"p":"“Sorry,” I said."},{"p":"He shrugged. “I do it too. Just with engines.”"},{"p":"We crossed a stretch of road bordered by low trees and drainage ditches. A delivery truck passed in the other direction, and Dad’s car gave a small shiver in its wake."},{"p":"“Was that wind?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“Partly the truck pushing air around,” Dad said. “And partly this car being old enough to complain about everything.”"},{"p":"I watched the grass flatten and rise beside the road. It occurred to me that I’d been looking at flags, clouds, and windsocks for weeks without really paying attention to the people sitting beside me."},{"p":"“How’s your dad’s schedule?” I asked Beckett."},{"p":"He picked at the edge of his notebook. “They’ve got a big repair job at the shop. If they finish it, things might go back to normal.”"},{"p":"“What’s the job?”"},{"p":"“Fleet vans. Three of them. The parts were late.”"},{"p":"I knew enough about Dad’s library job to complain when he worked weekends. I didn’t know what it was like to have an entire family’s schedule depend on parts arriving for somebody else’s vans."},{"p":"“That sounds rough.”"},{"p":"“It’s fine.” Beckett looked out the window again. “I just don’t want to miss the sessions.”"},{"p":"Dad didn’t offer an inspirational speech. He just said, “Well, we can do next Saturday too, if your folks need it. We’ll check first.”"},{"p":"“Thanks, Mr. Burgess.”"},{"p":"At Hampton Roads Executive Airport, the parking lot was busy with people carrying flight bags and coffee cups. Imani was waiting near the classroom door, one sneaker braced against the wall. Rafe stood beside her trying to balance a pencil across his upper lip."},{"p":"“Don’t move,” Rafe told us. “I’m setting a record.”"},{"p":"The pencil fell when he said *record*."},{"p":"“Record for what?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“Shortest successful attempt.”"},{"p":"Imani held up her notebook. “Mara moved the lesson inside first. Winds are gusty, and she wants us to work through the plan before anybody goes near an airplane.”"},{"p":"Rafe picked up the pencil. “Also, Lincoln brought a chauffeur.”"},{"p":"“My dad.”"},{"p":"“Very exclusive service.”"},{"p":"“He’s going to charge you for jokes like that,” I said."},{"p":"Inside, Mara had taped a large airport diagram to the whiteboard. Beside it were three cards marked *wind*, *runway*, and *limits*. She was checking something on a tablet when we entered."},{"p":"“Good,” she said. “Everyone made it. Today we’re going to talk about a decision pilots sometimes wish they could make with a single number.”"},{"p":"“A test score?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“A crosswind component.”"},{"p":"Beckett pulled out his notebook. I sat beside him, grateful that he hadn’t made a big deal out of the ride."},{"p":"Mara drew a line representing a runway and a second arrow showing wind coming from an angle."},{"p":"“A reported wind direction tells us where the wind comes *from*,” she said. “We care about the part pushing across the runway, but we also care about the part blowing along it. And we care about gusts, runway conditions, the airplane, and the pilot.”"},{"p":"“So if the crosswind number is okay, we go?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not automatically.” She tapped the card marked *limits*. “A calculation is information. It doesn’t make the decision for you.”"},{"p":"She handed each of us a worksheet with three imaginary weather situations. The first was straightforward: a steady wind mostly along the runway. The second came from the side with stronger gusts. The third had a lower reported wind but a note about shifting direction."},{"p":"We worked in pairs. Beckett and I disagreed immediately."},{"p":"“You can’t call the second one okay just because the average is lower than the third,” he said."},{"p":"“I didn’t.”"},{"p":"“You circled *go*.”"},{"p":"“I circled *maybe go*.”"},{"p":"“That’s still a circle around go.”"},{"p":"I looked down. He had a point. Again."},{"p":"Mara stopped by our table. “What’s the argument?”"},{"p":"“The second situation has gusts,” Beckett said, “and we don’t know enough about the airplane or the pilot.”"},{"p":"“Good observation. Lincoln?”"},{"p":"“I was treating it like a math problem. If the number worked, I thought that settled it.”"},{"p":"“Would it?”"},{"p":"“No.” I rubbed out my circle until the paper went fuzzy. “We’d need the actual conditions, aircraft guidance, and the instructor’s judgment. And a plan if the wind changes.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “The gusts matter. Wind speed can change quickly, and a safe decision takes more than one number.”"},{"p":"Across the room, Rafe raised his hand."},{"p":"“Is *stay inside and eat snacks* an approved answer?”"},{"p":"“It can be,” Mara said, “but you still have to finish the worksheet.”"},{"p":"By lunchtime, the wind outside had begun shoving the tall grass into waves. We stood at the classroom window while Mara checked the current reports and spoke with another instructor. A small airplane taxied past, moving slowly, its control surfaces shifting as the pilot worked the controls on the ground."},{"p":"“Will we fly?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not with me today,” Mara said. “Conditions are beyond what I want for your lesson. We can use the time to practice planning.”"},{"p":"I felt the familiar drop in my stomach. Another Saturday, another cancellation. I’d already pictured myself flying over the roads and fields, correcting for drift like I’d practiced. I could practically feel the headset on my ears."},{"p":"Beckett gave me a sideways glance. “You okay?”"},{"p":"“Not thrilled.”"},{"p":"“Me neither.”"},{"p":"I looked at him. “You were excited?”"},{"p":"“Obviously. I brought my good notebook.”"},{"p":"His good notebook had a grease stain on the cover and a corner that looked chewed."},{"p":"“That one’s the good one?”"},{"p":"“Don’t insult it.”"},{"p":"Mara gave us a new assignment: design a short local practice-flight plan, then explain what would make us change or cancel it. We couldn’t use *because Mara says so* as our entire answer, although Rafe tried."},{"p":"When Dad returned to collect us, Beckett was still bent over the diagram, arguing with Imani about where to put a turning point. I waited until he finished writing before I tapped his shoulder."},{"p":"“Ready?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.” He slipped the notebook into his bag. “Hey. Thanks for the ride.”"},{"p":"“You already said that.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"On the drive home, we talked about whether the academy’s ancient vending machine was deliberately keeping our dollar bills. Dad asked what we’d learned. Beckett explained wind components with more hand gestures than I’d ever seen him use."},{"p":"I didn’t interrupt him once."},{"p":"That might have been the hardest exercise of the day."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twelve — The Weather Between Us","blocks":[{"p":"The trouble started with a message that wasn’t meant for me."},{"p":"It appeared in our group chat at 7:14 on Tuesday evening, just as I was trying to finish a science assignment about forces."},{"p":"*Beckett: If Lincoln draws one more circle around GO, I’m going to eat the worksheet.*"},{"p":"A second later:"},{"p":"*Beckett: wrong chat*"},{"p":"Then:"},{"p":"*Rafe: too late. screenshot acquired.*"},{"p":"I stared at the screen. It was supposed to be a joke. I knew that. Beckett had been making jokes about my worksheet since Saturday, and I’d laughed at most of them. But seeing it written down without his face or his voice made it feel different."},{"p":"I typed *Glad I can entertain you*, then deleted it. I typed *Maybe you should worry about your own flying*, then deleted that too."},{"p":"Finally I put the phone facedown and tried to answer a question about balanced forces. The irony was not lost on me."},{"p":"Mom found me twenty minutes later staring at the same paragraph."},{"p":"“Homework going well?”"},{"p":"“Spectacularly.”"},{"p":"She pulled out the chair across from me. “That sounded like a no.”"},{"p":"“It’s just Beckett.”"},{"p":"“What’s just Beckett?”"},{"p":"I showed her the message. She read it and handed back the phone."},{"p":"“That would sting,” she said."},{"p":"“It’s not a big deal.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"She got up to rinse her mug. I hated that she hadn’t argued with me. If she had, I could have insisted I was fine and felt very mature about it."},{"p":"“Do you think he’s being a jerk?” I asked."},{"p":"“I think he made a joke you didn’t enjoy.”"},{"p":"“That’s the same thing.”"},{"p":"“Sometimes. Not always.” She set the mug on the rack. “What do you want him to know?”"},{"p":"I didn’t answer. I wasn’t sure. I wanted him to know that I was trying. I wanted him to remember I’d helped him get to class. I wanted him to admit that I was getting better at flying. Those were three different things, and only one of them had anything to do with his joke."},{"p":"My phone buzzed again."},{"p":"*Imani: Lincoln, you alive?*"},{"p":"*Rafe: if not, can I have his model airplane?*"},{"p":"I wrote *Alive. Working on homework.*"},{"p":"Beckett didn’t say anything."},{"p":"On Thursday, we had an afternoon academy workshop. Dad dropped me off before heading to the library. Imani and Rafe were already inside, building something from strips of colored tape and paper arrows. Beckett arrived five minutes later with a shoebox tucked under one arm."},{"p":"“What’s in there?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“A fan.”"},{"p":"“You brought a fan in a shoebox?”"},{"p":"“It’s a small fan.”"},{"p":"“Does it have feelings?”"},{"p":"Beckett ignored him and set the box on the table. He glanced at me."},{"p":"“Hey.”"},{"p":"“Hey.”"},{"p":"He opened the box. I looked at the wall."},{"p":"Mara came in carrying a roll of masking tape and a stack of index cards. She surveyed the materials spread across the table."},{"p":"“Should I ask?”"},{"p":"“Wind laboratory,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Low budget,” Rafe added."},{"p":"“Very low,” Beckett said."},{"p":"Mara spotted a fray in the fan’s power cord and set it aside without plugging it in. “This stays off until somebody qualified checks it. We can use the hand fans from the supply cabinet.”"},{"p":"Beckett’s ears went pink. “I didn’t notice that.”"},{"p":"“That’s why we inspect equipment,” Mara said. She wasn’t angry. Somehow that made it worse."},{"p":"We built a tabletop experiment with a paper airplane, a strip of yarn suspended as a wind indicator, and a hand fan. The idea was to see how changing the direction of moving air affected the model’s path. It wasn’t a precise simulation of an aircraft in flight, Mara warned us. The air around the classroom was messy, the paper airplane was inconsistent, and our fan wasn’t a calibrated instrument."},{"p":"“Then what’s the point?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“To observe what happens and ask better questions,” Mara said. “Not to pretend this is the sky.”"},{"p":"Imani launched the paper airplane. It sailed two feet, caught a sideways puff from the fan, and struck Rafe in the chest."},{"p":"“I’m filing a report,” he said."},{"p":"“Pilot error,” Beckett replied."},{"p":"“Hostile weather.”"},{"p":"“Hostile pilot,” Imani said."},{"p":"I laughed, then caught Beckett looking at me. He turned back to the model."},{"p":"We took turns. The results were ridiculous in different ways. One airplane looped into a chair. Another landed in Mara’s open tote bag. A third, folded by Beckett with great care, flew straight until the fan caught it and sent it spinning onto the floor."},{"p":"“Clearly a terrible design,” I said before I could stop myself."},{"p":"Beckett’s head snapped up. “It flew fine without the fan.”"},{"p":"“That’s the point, isn’t it?”"},{"p":"“Yeah, and maybe you’d notice if you weren’t trying to win every experiment.”"},{"p":"The room went quiet enough that I heard the hand fan clicking as Imani set it down."},{"p":"“I was joking,” I said."},{"p":"“So was I on Tuesday.”"},{"p":"He looked surprised he’d said it. So was I."},{"p":"Mara was at the supply cabinet. She didn’t jump into the conversation, but she didn’t pretend she couldn’t hear us either."},{"p":"“Take five minutes,” she said. “Then come back and record what actually happened, not what you hoped would happen.”"},{"p":"Rafe and Imani went to refill their water bottles. Beckett stayed at the table, pressing a crease flat on his paper airplane."},{"p":"“I wasn’t trying to make you look stupid,” he said."},{"p":"“Could’ve fooled me.”"},{"p":"“I said it was the wrong chat.”"},{"p":"“That doesn’t mean you didn’t say it.”"},{"p":"He ran a thumb along the folded wing. “Fair.”"},{"p":"I leaned against the table. “I know I get ahead of myself. You don’t have to remind me every time.”"},{"p":"“You remind me every time I get something wrong.”"},{"p":"I started to object. Then I remembered the wind tunnel, the runway diagram, and about a dozen things I’d said since June."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “I probably do.”"},{"p":"Beckett set down the airplane. “I wasn’t making fun of you for asking questions. I was making fun of the circle.”"},{"p":"“It felt like the same thing.”"},{"p":"He nodded. Neither of us apologized immediately. It would’ve been easier if one of us had been completely wrong."},{"p":"“I shouldn’t have sent it,” he said at last."},{"p":"“I shouldn’t have called your airplane terrible.”"},{"p":"“It was terrible.”"},{"p":"“It had a strong first two feet.”"},{"p":"He snorted."},{"p":"When the others returned, Imani looked from one of us to the other. “Are we allowed to talk about airplanes again?”"},{"p":"“Under supervision,” I said."},{"p":"Mara gave us a fresh observation sheet. We wrote down the conditions for each attempt, including the direction of the fan, the type of fold, and whether the paper airplane had started straight. It turned out we couldn’t compare the results very well because we’d changed several things at once."},{"p":"“So the experiment’s useless?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Not useless,” Imani said. “It tells us what to fix next time.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Fair conclusion. If we don’t know enough, we gather more information—or choose the safer option.”"},{"p":"After the workshop, I walked outside with Beckett while we waited for our rides. The late-afternoon sun had turned the parked cars into little ovens. Across the airport, a windsock hung at an angle, shifting as the breeze changed."},{"p":"“Dad says the vans might be finished Friday,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“That’s good.”"},{"p":"“Maybe. They found another problem.”"},{"p":"“Of course they did.”"},{"p":"He gave me a look."},{"p":"“Sorry,” I said. “I mean, that’s frustrating.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.” He shifted his backpack. “Your dad doesn’t have to drive me forever.”"},{"p":"“He knows.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"We watched a small airplane roll past on a taxiway. Its wings rocked a little as a gust crossed the open pavement."},{"p":"“I keep thinking there’s supposed to be a trick,” I said. “Like if I understand the wind well enough, I’ll know exactly what to do every time.”"},{"p":"Beckett picked a pebble off the edge of the sidewalk and tossed it toward a patch of weeds. It landed short."},{"p":"“My dad says half his job is figuring out what he doesn’t know yet,” he said. “He hates it when a customer tells him what part to replace before he’s even looked.”"},{"p":"“That sounds familiar.”"},{"p":"“Don’t start.”"},{"p":"A car horn sounded from the pickup lane. Dad had arrived, one hand lifted above the steering wheel."},{"p":"“Want a ride?” I asked."},{"p":"“Sure.”"},{"p":"We started toward the car. The breeze pushed at our backs, carrying the smell of hot pavement and cut grass."},{"p":"For once, I didn’t try to guess how fast it was blowing."}]},{"t":"Chapter Thirteen — The Crab Walk","blocks":[{"p":"On Saturday morning, Dad burned the first pancake and blamed the skillet."},{"p":"The skillet had been in our kitchen longer than I had. It had survived fourteen years of breakfast without developing a grudge, but Dad seemed convinced it had started one overnight."},{"p":"“Uneven heating,” he said, scraping a black circle into the trash."},{"p":"“Uneven attention,” Mom said from the table."},{"p":"I was reading the weather on my phone. Outside our Virginia Beach kitchen window, the maple leaves barely moved. At Hampton Roads Executive, though, the latest report showed a breeze that wasn’t straight down the runway."},{"p":"“Is that a problem?” Dad asked when I read it aloud."},{"p":"“Not automatically.” I put down the phone. “Depends on the runway, what the wind’s doing when we get there, the airplane, and what Mara thinks is appropriate.”"},{"p":"Mom looked up from her coffee. “That sounded suspiciously like a complete answer.”"},{"p":"“I’m experimenting with them.”"},{"p":"Dad slid a pancake onto my plate. This one was pale and lopsided but edible."},{"p":"“Don’t let the fame go to your head,” he said."},{"p":"At the airport, I found Beckett standing beside the classroom door with a long cardboard box under one arm. The box had a shipping label on it and a corner that looked as if a dog had tried to eat it."},{"p":"“What’s that?” I asked."},{"p":"“Something that will change aviation forever.”"},{"p":"“Another fan with exposed wiring?”"},{"p":"“Wow. We reconcile one argument and you’re already impossible again.”"},{"p":"He opened the box. Inside were two cheap plastic model airplanes and a roll of blue painter’s tape."},{"p":"“Forty-three dollars of scientific greatness,” he said."},{"p":"“Your dad get the van back?”"},{"p":"“One of them. Other one’s still at the shop.” He said it casually, but I noticed the way he looked toward the parking lot. “Mom’s picking me up today.”"},{"p":"I didn’t ask which car she’d use. A month ago I might have."},{"p":"Imani arrived with a notebook full of neatly ruled columns. Rafe came in behind her, holding a breakfast sandwich with enough sauce to qualify as a structural hazard."},{"p":"“Are we flying?” he asked."},{"p":"“That’s what I want to know,” I said."},{"p":"“Good morning to you too,” Mara said, appearing behind us."},{"p":"She had a clipboard in one hand and a pair of sunglasses perched on her head. She looked at the box, then at Rafe’s sandwich."},{"p":"“One of those is staying outside the airplane,” she said."},{"p":"Rafe looked offended. “The sandwich is nearly finished.”"},{"p":"“I was talking about the box.”"},{"p":"We crowded around the weather display. Mara pointed out the reported wind, then the runway orientation. She made us work through what the numbers meant rather than announcing the answer."},{"p":"“The wind isn’t a wall,” she said. “That report describes one place at one time. We still need to look outside and keep checking.”"},{"p":"A few minutes later, she and I walked toward the Cessna. The sky was bright enough to make me squint. A tug rattled past towing an airplane toward the hangars, and the scent of cut grass came from the field beyond the apron."},{"p":"I carried the checklist. Mara carried the expression that meant I’d better use it."},{"p":"The preflight went slowly. I liked that it went slowly now. Not every second, obviously. There was still a part of me that wanted to climb in, start the engine, and get airborne before anyone could invent another reason to cancel. But I’d learned what happened when I let that part do all the thinking."},{"p":"At the left wing, I paused over a fastener near an inspection panel. Mara checked it with me before we continued."},{"p":"“You can ask,” she said."},{"p":"“I know. I’m deciding whether I have a question.”"},{"p":"“That’s allowed.”"},{"p":"By the time we finished the checklist and settled into the cockpit, my shirt was sticking to my back. The headset made my ears feel enormous. Mara handled the radio work and the portions of the operation that were hers to manage. I followed along, reading the checklist items aloud and confirming them as instructed."},{"p":"During taxi, she reminded me that wind mattered on the ground, too. The ailerons and elevator weren’t just decorations once the wheels touched pavement. Their position could help the airplane respond appropriately to wind while taxiing, according to the manufacturer’s guidance and the actual conditions."},{"p":"I glanced at the windsock. It shifted, relaxed, and lifted again."},{"p":"“Like it can’t make up its mind,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s giving us information. We have to decide what to do with it.”"},{"p":"Mara supervised the takeoff, and we climbed toward our practice area. The neighborhoods gave way to patches of green and silver roads. Far off, a line of water flashed in the sun."},{"p":"I never got tired of the view. Even the shopping centers looked interesting from above, like somebody had built a miniature city and forgotten to put people in it."},{"p":"Once we were established at a safe practice altitude, Mara took us through a few familiar exercises. She had me hold a heading and altitude while watching the horizon and making small corrections. I still had a habit of staring at the instrument panel when I felt uncertain. She reminded me to keep my eyes moving and to look outside for other traffic."},{"p":"Then she pointed toward a long straight road below us."},{"p":"“Tell me what you notice about our movement relative to that road.”"},{"p":"I looked from the nose of the airplane to the road and back. The nose pointed one way, but our path over the ground seemed to be sliding sideways."},{"p":"“We’re drifting.”"},{"p":"“Which direction?”"},{"p":"I answered, then watched a little longer. The first answer hadn’t been quite right. I corrected it."},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Good. Now let’s choose a reference and work on maintaining a ground track. I’ll guide you through the correction.”"},{"p":"I made a small heading adjustment into the wind. The road shifted toward the center of my view, then stayed more nearly where I expected it."},{"p":"“So we’re flying sideways?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not exactly. The airplane is flying forward through the surrounding air. That moving air carries us relative to the ground. We point the nose somewhat into the wind so our path over the ground goes where we intend.”"},{"p":"“Like a crab walking.”"},{"p":"“That’s why pilots call it crabbing.”"},{"p":"“Crabs are going to be unbearable when they hear about this.”"},{"p":"She laughed once, then tapped the edge of the chart on her lap. “Keep watching your reference.”"},{"p":"I did. For several seconds it worked. Then I grew pleased with myself, looked down to check whether the heading indicator agreed with my excellent flying, and let the airplane drift off the track again."},{"p":"“Eyes outside, then cross-check your instruments,” Mara said. “Make small corrections.”"},{"p":"I eased back toward the correction. Too much. The road moved the other way."},{"p":"“Smaller,” she said."},{"p":"I tried again."},{"p":"The next attempt was better. Not perfect. Not even particularly graceful. But I could see why it was better, which felt different from having somebody tell me it was."},{"p":"We repeated the exercise with another reference. The wind didn’t stay perfectly steady, and neither did I. Mara showed me how to notice a change, check what was happening, and adjust without wrestling the controls."},{"p":"At one point I started turning too far, stopped myself, and said, “That was too much.”"},{"p":"“What told you?”"},{"p":"“The ground track changed more than I wanted.”"},{"p":"“Good observation.”"},{"p":"I wanted her to say *excellent flying*. She didn’t. I was surprised to discover that *good observation* was enough."},{"p":"On the way back, the air got a little bumpier. Nothing dramatic happened. My stomach floated once, which I hated, and Mara explained what she was noticing as she monitored the conditions. We returned to the airport, and she flew the landing while I watched the relationship between the airplane’s heading and its path toward the runway. She didn’t ask me to perform a crosswind landing. I wasn’t ready, and we both knew it."},{"p":"After shutdown, I took off the headset and discovered a flattened ridge of hair across the top of my head."},{"p":"“Spectacular,” I said, catching my reflection in the window."},{"p":"“Your hair?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“My professional pilot appearance.”"},{"p":"“It’s coming along.”"},{"p":"Inside, Beckett wanted to know whether I’d learned to fly backward."},{"p":"“Sideways,” I said."},{"p":"“Even better.”"},{"p":"Imani opened her notebook. “Did the wind stay the same the whole time?”"},{"p":"“No. And I kept correcting too much until I actually watched what was happening.”"},{"p":"She wrote something down."},{"p":"“Are you taking notes on my failures?”"},{"p":"“Only the educational ones.”"},{"p":"I tried to snatch the notebook. She moved it out of reach without looking up."},{"p":"Across the room, Rafe held up one of Beckett’s plastic airplanes. Its wing had come loose."},{"p":"“Greatness,” he announced, “has suffered a mechanical setback.”"},{"p":"Beckett groaned and went to rescue it."},{"p":"I stood there with the smell of warm engine oil still in my nose and a faint headset mark across my forehead. The flight hadn’t made me feel fearless. It had made me want to go back and try again."},{"p":"That seemed better."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fourteen — A Forecast for Saturday","blocks":[{"p":"Rafe’s birthday invitation and the weekend forecast arrived in the same week, and they did not get along."},{"p":"Rafe had sent the invitation. The forecast hadn’t asked my permission, which made it harder to argue with."},{"p":"Rafe was turning fourteen in two weeks and had decided that the proper way to celebrate was to invite six people to a miniature-golf place with a pirate ship, an arcade, and, according to its website, “the most exciting indoor volcano on the coast.”"},{"p":"“The volcano is not indoors,” I told him at lunch on Tuesday. “It’s a painted mountain with a fog machine.”"},{"p":"“You’ve been?”"},{"p":"“I was nine.”"},{"p":"“Then you’re due for another expedition.”"},{"p":"Imani studied the invitation. “Why does it say formal attire optional?”"},{"p":"“Because I want someone to show up in a tuxedo.”"},{"p":"Beckett took a chip from Rafe’s lunch tray. “Not happening.”"},{"p":"“You’d look great in a tuxedo.”"},{"p":"“I’d look like somebody forced me to wear a tuxedo.”"},{"p":"The party was Saturday afternoon, the same day our academy group had a scheduled outdoor wind-observation project. We were supposed to set up a simple observation station, record changing wind conditions, and compare our notes with the airport’s reported weather. Mara had made it clear that the project could be moved or canceled if the conditions weren’t suitable for students to be outside."},{"p":"I had already told Rafe I would come. Then Mom reminded me that we had promised to help Dad with the library’s used-book sale that morning."},{"p":"“It’s one hour,” she said."},{"p":"“It’s never one hour. Last time we were there until somebody found a box of encyclopedias from 1987.”"},{"p":"“Somebody had to carry them.”"},{"p":"“That somebody was me.”"},{"p":"“You’re welcome for the character development.”"},{"p":"By Thursday evening I had managed to fit all three events into a schedule on a piece of notebook paper. The plan required Dad’s book sale to finish exactly on time, Mom to drive as if every traffic light in Virginia Beach had agreed to cooperate, and Rafe’s party to begin seventeen minutes late."},{"p":"I thought it was a very good plan."},{"p":"Dad examined it over dinner."},{"p":"“What’s this dotted line?”"},{"p":"“Travel time.”"},{"p":"“You’ve given us eleven minutes to get from the library to the airport.”"},{"p":"“Ten, actually. The extra minute is a buffer.”"},{"p":"Dad pushed his glasses up his nose. “How generous.”"},{"p":"“Could you just look at the rest?”"},{"p":"He did. Then he pointed at the last line. “You have yourself arriving at Rafe’s party at two-thirty. It starts at two.”"},{"p":"“Rafe said late was fine.”"},{"p":"“Did he?”"},{"p":"“He said, and I quote, ‘If you’re late, you’re walking the plank.’”"},{"p":"“That sounds less fine.”"},{"p":"Mom put down her fork. “Lincoln, it’s okay to tell somebody you can’t do everything.”"},{"p":"“I can do everything if nobody changes anything.”"},{"p":"Dad and Mom looked at each other. I knew that look. It was the look adults used when they were trying not to say the obvious thing at the same time."},{"p":"“What?” I asked."},{"p":"“Nothing,” Dad said."},{"p":"“That’s the worst kind of nothing.”"},{"p":"On Friday morning, a weather alert appeared on my phone. A passing front was expected to bring showers and gusty winds to the area on Saturday. The details would change, but the forecast was enough to make the outdoor project uncertain."},{"p":"I sent a screenshot to the group chat."},{"p":"**LINCOLN:** might solve the schedule problem"},{"p":"**RAFE:** weather has been hired as my party planner"},{"p":"**IMANI:** it might cancel the observation project. it doesn’t solve your book sale"},{"p":"**BECKETT:** let him have this for eight seconds"},{"p":"At the academy that afternoon, Mara had us compare several weather forecasts and talk about why they differed. One model showed the strongest winds arriving before lunch. Another put them later in the day. The aviation forecast products had their own time windows and limitations."},{"p":"“Which one is right?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“We don’t know yet,” Mara said. “Forecasts can change. We’ll check again before deciding.”"},{"p":"“So we pick the one we like?” I said."},{"p":"“You could. You’d be choosing a comfortable story, not making a sound decision.”"},{"p":"I looked at the time on my phone and thought about the three appointments crammed onto my schedule."},{"p":"Mara showed us how to read the forecast wind direction and speed, then how to look for changes over time. She made us explain the difference between a steady wind and gusts, and why an airport observation wasn’t guaranteed to describe every part of a nearby flight path."},{"p":"“Can we do the project Saturday?” I asked."},{"p":"“We’ll decide based on the actual conditions and the latest information. The answer might be yes, modified, postponed, or no.”"},{"p":"Four possibilities. My schedule had room for one, preferably the one I’d already chosen."},{"p":"After class, Beckett found me at the water fountain."},{"p":"“Your face is doing that thing,” he said."},{"p":"“What thing?”"},{"p":"“The thing where you look like somebody rearranged your toolbox.”"},{"p":"“I don’t have a toolbox.”"},{"p":"“Exactly. Imagine how upset you’d be.”"},{"p":"I told him about Saturday. He listened while I explained the plan, including the eleven-minute drive that Dad had questioned."},{"p":"“You could skip the book sale,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I promised Dad.”"},{"p":"“Then tell Rafe you’ll be late.”"},{"p":"“I already told him I’d be there.”"},{"p":"“Then tell Mara you might miss the project.”"},{"p":"“I signed up for it.”"},{"p":"Beckett leaned against the wall. “You know those are all sentences you can say out loud, right?”"},{"p":"“Thank you for the revolutionary advice.”"},{"p":"“Anytime.”"},{"p":"I went home and made a new schedule. This one had wider margins and two possible routes. It was still terrible."},{"p":"Saturday arrived gray and damp. At eight in the morning, I helped Dad unload donated books at the library. The boxes smelled like dust, cardboard, and somebody’s basement. I carried mysteries, cookbooks, three atlases, and a stack of paperback romances with covers that made Dad cough loudly every time I picked one up."},{"p":"By nine-thirty, rain was tapping against the windows. The outdoor academy project was postponed. Mara sent a message explaining that the group would review the weather observations remotely and conduct the field activity another day."},{"p":"I stared at the message for a full ten seconds."},{"p":"Dad watched me over a box of gardening manuals. “Good news?”"},{"p":"“Sort of. The project is postponed.”"},{"p":"“Then you have some breathing room.”"},{"p":"“Except Rafe’s party.”"},{"p":"“We’ll get you there.”"},{"p":"I looked at the towers of books around us. A volunteer was asking Dad where the children’s section should go. Another box had split open, scattering paperbacks across the floor."},{"p":"“We’re not going to finish by eleven,” I said."},{"p":"“Nope.”"},{"p":"Dad didn’t turn it into a lecture. He handed me a roll of tape."},{"p":"At eleven-fifteen, I messaged Rafe."},{"p":"**LINCOLN:** helping dad longer than expected. might be late. sorry"},{"p":"His reply came before I finished taping the box."},{"p":"**RAFE:** understood. pirate law allows a 30 minute grace period"},{"p":"Then:"},{"p":"**RAFE:** after that you have to walk the plank"},{"p":"I smiled at my phone. A minute later, Imani sent a picture of the forecast with a note about how the strongest gusts had arrived earlier than one prediction had suggested. Beckett replied with a photo of his repaired model airplane and the words **STILL AIRWORTHY IN MY IMAGINATION**."},{"p":"We finished the book sale setup shortly after noon. Dad bought me a hot dog from the volunteer table. The bun was soggy and the mustard had escaped onto my wrist."},{"p":"“Great planning,” I told him."},{"p":"“Excellent execution,” he said."},{"p":"On the drive to the party, the rain faded to a mist. The trees still shook in the wind. I watched the branches bend and straighten, each one responding a little differently."},{"p":"“You’ve been quiet,” Mom said from the passenger seat."},{"p":"“I’m thinking.”"},{"p":"“About?”"},{"p":"“Forecasts. And how I made a schedule like the weather and the traffic and everybody else had signed a contract.”"},{"p":"Dad glanced at me in the mirror. “They usually refuse to sign.”"},{"p":"“Yeah. I’m noticing.”"},{"p":"We reached the miniature-golf place twenty-six minutes after the party started. Rafe was waiting near the pirate ship, wearing a paper captain’s hat. He handed me a plastic sword without asking where I’d been."},{"p":"“You’re just in time,” he said. “Beckett has already lost three golf balls.”"},{"p":"“Two,” Beckett called from behind a fake cannon. “The third one was stolen by the volcano.”"},{"p":"Imani appeared beside him, holding a scorecard. “The volcano has an impressive record.”"},{"p":"I took the sword. It bent in the middle."},{"p":"“Is this the plank-walking equipment?”"},{"p":"“That’s for later,” Rafe said. “Right now we need you to defeat a windmill.”"},{"p":"I looked at the miniature-golf course. One hole had a rotating wooden windmill with a narrow opening between its blades. The motor turned at an uneven speed, and the ball kept bouncing off the frame."},{"p":"“What’s the trick?” I asked."},{"p":"Rafe grinned. “Timing.”"},{"p":"I waited for the opening, took a gentle swing, and watched the ball roll directly into a puddle beside the windmill."},{"p":"Beckett laughed so hard he had to sit down."},{"p":"I laughed too. Then I fished the ball out and tried again."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fifteen — The Other End of the Windsock","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing I noticed about the windsock was that it wasn’t pointing where I expected."},{"p":"It was Tuesday afternoon at Hampton Roads Executive, and the parking lot felt like somebody had opened an oven. The asphalt smelled hot. A dragonfly kept hitting the window of the classroom, making a noise like a tiny, angry helicopter."},{"p":"On my phone, the wind report from the airport had shown a direction that made me think the windsock would stretch almost straight across the runway. But the orange fabric outside was drooping at an angle, changing its mind every few seconds."},{"p":"“It’s broken,” I said."},{"p":"Imani didn’t look up from her notebook. “The wind?”"},{"p":"“The sock.”"},{"p":"“Bold accusation.”"},{"p":"Beckett pressed his forehead to the glass. “Maybe it’s just tired.”"},{"p":"Mara came in carrying four clipboards and a plastic bag full of clothespins. “Nobody accuse the windsock of anything until we’ve taken measurements.”"},{"p":"She handed each of us a clipboard. The sheets had columns for time, observed direction, approximate strength, and anything unusual we noticed. We were going outside, she explained, to watch conditions change rather than treating a single report like a permanent description of the air."},{"p":"“Do we get the clothespins?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“They’re for a separate exercise.”"},{"p":"“That’s what people say when they’re hiding the good equipment.”"},{"p":"We walked to the approved observation area beside the academy building, well clear of the movement areas. Heat rose from the pavement in wavy lines. Beyond the fence, an airplane taxied slowly past, its propeller flashing in the sun. A loose piece of grass skittered along the ground and stopped against Beckett’s shoe."},{"p":"Mara checked the time. “Two minutes. Watch first. Write second.”"},{"p":"I held my pencil ready anyway."},{"p":"At the beginning, the windsock pointed toward the far corner of the field. Then it sagged. A moment later it filled again, a little farther around. I could hear wind brushing through the grass, but I couldn’t feel much of it on my face until I stepped out of the building’s shadow."},{"p":"“That’s annoying,” I said."},{"p":"“Which part?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“The part where it won’t stay still long enough for me to decide what it’s doing.”"},{"p":"“Try describing what it’s doing instead.”"},{"p":"I wrote down three observations. The last one was *variable*. It felt like cheating until Mara nodded at my notes."},{"p":"Imani had drawn little arrows next to each time. Rafe had added a note that said *one extremely confident dragonfly, direction unknown*."},{"p":"“Useful,” Mara told him, “if we ever establish a dragonfly advisory service.”"},{"p":"When we went inside, she clipped two lengths of string to the edge of a cardboard runway model. A desk fan stood at one end of the table. The clothespins were weights, so the strings wouldn’t blow all over the room."},{"p":"“The airport report matters. So does the windsock, and what you see outside. But no single observation describes the wind everywhere.”"},{"p":"She turned on the fan. The strings streamed sideways. When Beckett moved a cardboard box between the fan and the runway, one string fluttered while the other barely moved."},{"p":"“Buildings and other obstacles can change the airflow nearby,” Mara said. “And gusts can change what the airplane does.”"},{"p":"I watched the string nearest the box flick left, then right. “So the sock wasn’t wrong.”"},{"p":"“It might have been showing something different from what you expected,” Mara said. “That’s not the same thing.”"},{"p":"Beckett made a tiny bow toward the window. “Our apologies to the accused.”"},{"p":"Mara gave us a scenario. The reported wind favored one runway, but gusts were increasing and the direction was shifting. We were to list what we’d want to know before deciding whether a training flight should proceed."},{"p":"I wrote *crosswind component* immediately. Then *gusts*. Then I stopped."},{"p":"“Airplane limitations?” Imani suggested."},{"p":"“Pilot experience,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Runway conditions,” Rafe added. “And whether the instructor likes us.”"},{"p":"“You won’t find that one in the airplane’s handbook,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Could be,” Rafe muttered."},{"p":"I stared at my list. It had been easier when I thought there was one right number. If the wind was under that number, go. If it was over, stay. But we’d spent weeks discovering how often the real answer depended on more than one thing."},{"p":"“What if the wind changes after we’re up?” I asked."},{"p":"“Then the plan can change,” Mara said. “We monitor conditions and make decisions throughout the flight. That’s one reason we brief alternatives before leaving the ground.”"},{"p":"I looked out at the windsock again. It had settled into a new angle. Nothing dramatic. Just a piece of fabric doing exactly what the air told it to do."},{"p":"After class, I found Beckett at the bike rack, working the combination lock on a chain that seemed determined to eat his shoelace. Dad had offered him a ride, but Beckett said his older sister was picking him up."},{"p":"“Your sister still mad about the group chat?” I asked."},{"p":"He straightened. “She wasn’t mad about the chat. She was mad because I told her I’d be ready at four and I wasn’t.”"},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"He went back to the lock."},{"p":"I shifted my backpack higher. “I thought about that joke I made.”"},{"p":"“Which one? You have a whole collection.”"},{"p":"“The one about your ride.”"},{"p":"He tugged the chain loose. “Yeah.”"},{"p":"“I knew it wasn’t funny after I sent it. I should’ve said something then.”"},{"p":"Beckett wound the chain around his handlebars. “Probably.”"},{"p":"A car horn sounded from the road. Neither of us moved."},{"p":"“I wasn’t trying to make your family the joke,” I said."},{"p":"“I know. That’s why it was weird.”"},{"p":"He looked up at me, freckles bright against his sunburned nose. “My mom works late. My sister has her own stuff. Sometimes getting here is complicated. I don’t really want to explain that every time somebody asks.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"A white hatchback pulled into the lot. Beckett lifted his bike and rolled it toward the car, then stopped."},{"p":"“Your runway calculation was wrong, by the way,” he said."},{"p":"I blinked. “What?”"},{"p":"“The one from class. You used the steady wind speed when Mara asked about the gusts.”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth, then closed it. “I did?”"},{"p":"“Yep.”"},{"p":"“Thanks.”"},{"p":"“Don’t get used to it.”"},{"p":"He grinned, just a little, and pushed his bike away."},{"p":"At home I checked my worksheet. He was right. I had written the smaller number because it was the first one I saw."},{"p":"I crossed out the number, worked the problem again, and left the messy correction visible. Then I took a picture and sent it to Beckett."},{"p":"**LINCOLN:** you were right. horrible development"},{"p":"His reply arrived six minutes later."},{"p":"**BECKETT:** frame this message"},{"p":"I didn’t. But I saved the worksheet."}]},{"t":"Chapter Sixteen — The Approach We Didn’t Fly","blocks":[{"p":"On Thursday, Mara put a folded airport diagram on the table between us and said, “Tell me what you think today’s lesson is.”"},{"p":"I looked at the diagram. “Taxiways?”"},{"p":"“Try again.”"},{"p":"“Learning to fold paper?”"},{"p":"She raised one eyebrow."},{"p":"“Wind,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s a safer guess.”"},{"p":"Outside, the sky was mostly blue, with bright clouds stacked along the horizon. A breeze kept lifting the corner of Mara’s kneeboard. It looked like a day built for flying."},{"p":"Our lesson was supposed to include a supervised flight in the practice area and, if conditions allowed, observing how Mara managed the airplane on approach and landing. I was especially interested in that last part. In my imagination, I could see the runway getting larger through the windshield while the airplane stayed perfectly lined up."},{"p":"Mara had already informed me that imagination was not an approved flight instrument."},{"p":"We checked the aircraft documents and weather information, then walked out to the Cessna. I performed the preflight inspection with Mara supervising, following the checklist and asking before touching anything I wasn’t sure about. The metal skin was warm under my fingers. Fuel trucks moved on the far side of the ramp, and somewhere behind us a mower rattled along the grass."},{"p":"The wind was coming across the runway at an angle. It wasn’t particularly strong, but the gusts were noticeable. Mara showed me how she estimated the crosswind component from the wind direction and runway heading, and reminded me that an airplane’s demonstrated crosswind value was not a magical dividing line between safe and unsafe."},{"p":"“It’s a crosswind component demonstrated during certification testing,” she said. “Not a guarantee that every pilot can handle that much wind—or that the airplane can’t handle more.”"},{"p":"“Which means we also care about who’s flying,” I said."},{"p":"“And the conditions, and the airplane, and the purpose of the flight.”"},{"p":"“Which means the answer is complicated.”"},{"p":"“Usually.”"},{"p":"We strapped in, worked through the checklist, and checked the latest available weather report. I repeated the wind direction and speed out loud. Mara asked me to compare it with what we’d reviewed inside."},{"p":"“It’s shifted,” I said. “And the gusts are higher.”"},{"p":"“Good catch.”"},{"p":"I waited for her to say we were going anyway."},{"p":"Instead, she took a moment to review our options. We could continue with a modified lesson if conditions remained appropriate, wait for another observation, or stop before engine start. She asked what I thought."},{"p":"I studied the diagram on my lap. The runway looked harmless as a pencil line. The wind arrow I’d drawn beside it looked harmless too. But neither one was the actual airplane moving through the actual air."},{"p":"“I want to fly,” I said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“I think we should wait for the next report.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “That’s reasonable.”"},{"p":"We waited in the parked cockpit with the engine off and the doors cracked for air. A bead of sweat slid down my back. Another pilot’s call crackled over the radio. Mara listened, then turned the volume down so we could talk."},{"p":"“What would a crosswind landing feel like?” I asked."},{"p":"“It depends on the wind and the airplane. In a steady crosswind, you might see the runway slightly off the nose while the airplane maintains its track toward it. Before touchdown, the airplane needs to be aligned with the runway, with drift controlled. Pilots use the appropriate technique and make adjustments as conditions change.”"},{"p":"“So you don’t just aim the nose at the middle and hope.”"},{"p":"“Hope is not much of a control input.”"},{"p":"I smiled, then looked toward the runway again."},{"p":"An airplane on final approach came into view. From where we sat, I couldn’t judge its alignment very well. It seemed to move sideways for a second, then disappeared behind the hangars before touchdown."},{"p":"“Was that a crab?” I asked."},{"p":"“Possibly. From here we can’t tell exactly what the pilot was doing.”"},{"p":"I liked that she said *can’t tell* instead of pretending she could."},{"p":"Mara checked the next weather update. The wind had shifted again, and the gusts were stronger than before. Mara read it, then folded the diagram."},{"p":"“We’re not going to launch this lesson,” she said."},{"p":"My stomach dropped. “At all?”"},{"p":"“Not today. We could conduct a different ground lesson, but the conditions aren’t a good match for what we planned to practice with you.”"},{"p":"I stared through the windshield. We’d done all that work. The airplane was right there. I could almost feel the yoke moving in my hands."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said, in a voice that didn’t sound okay."},{"p":"Mara didn’t congratulate me for being mature. She didn’t say disappointment was secretly wonderful. She opened her door and stepped onto the ramp."},{"p":"I followed her out. The wind pushed at the loose end of my shirt. We secured the airplane as directed, returned the equipment, and went inside."},{"p":"The others were in the classroom. Rafe looked up from a bag of pretzels."},{"p":"“Flight?” he asked."},{"p":"“Nope.”"},{"p":"“Pretzel?”"},{"p":"“Definitely.”"},{"p":"He tossed me the bag. I caught it badly, against my chest, and a few pretzels escaped onto the floor."},{"p":"“Smooth handling,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Crosswind,” I told him."},{"p":"Imani glanced at my face. “Weather change?”"},{"p":"“Gusts went up. We stayed on the ground.”"},{"p":"“Bummer.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"She moved her notebook so I could sit down. No speech. No lesson about how a canceled flight was a successful flight. Just room at the table and a pencil that actually had an eraser."},{"p":"Mara drew a runway on the whiteboard and gave us a new scenario. We had to plan an arrival with a changing crosswind, identify where we’d seek updated information, and decide what alternatives we’d brief before departure. She made it clear that the exercise wasn’t permission for students to attempt approaches on their own. It was practice in thinking ahead."},{"p":"Rafe volunteered to be the weather briefer and immediately gave his report in the voice of a game-show announcer."},{"p":"“CONGRATULATIONS, PILOTS! YOU’VE WON A FREE GUST!”"},{"p":"“Please never work for the weather service,” Imani said."},{"p":"Beckett drew an airplane that looked like a potato with wings. He insisted the potato was aerodynamically sound."},{"p":"For the first twenty minutes, I barely spoke. I was still thinking about the flight we hadn’t taken. Then Mara asked what we’d do if the crosswind became uncomfortable during an approach."},{"p":"“Keep going?” Rafe said, clearly joking."},{"p":"“Absolutely not,” Imani replied."},{"p":"I looked at the little airplane on the board. “A pilot could go around if the approach isn’t working out,” I said. “And if the conditions aren’t suitable, land somewhere else or wait, depending on the situation and the plan.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Exactly. A go-around is a normal maneuver, not a punishment. The point is to keep options available.”"},{"p":"She asked me to explain why. I did, using the runway diagram, the wind arrow, and the crooked potato airplane. I had to start over once because I mixed up the wind direction and the direction the wind was blowing toward."},{"p":"Nobody laughed. Beckett quietly turned the arrow around for me."},{"p":"After class, Dad picked me up in the library’s aging sedan. The air-conditioning made a noise like a playing card in bicycle spokes."},{"p":"“How was flying?” he asked."},{"p":"“We didn’t.”"},{"p":"He glanced over. “Mechanical problem?”"},{"p":"“Wind. It changed.”"},{"p":"“That’s disappointing.”"},{"p":"“It is.”"},{"p":"We waited at a traffic light while a cyclist struggled to keep a paper bag balanced in a front basket. A gust tipped the bag sideways. The cyclist stopped, fixed it, and waited for the light to change."},{"p":"“I still learned stuff,” I said."},{"p":"Dad nodded. “Want to tell me about it?”"},{"p":"“Only if you promise not to ask whether the plane can fly backward.”"},{"p":"“I make no promises.”"},{"p":"I explained the difference between heading and ground track. I drew an invisible runway in the air with my finger and nearly poked the dashboard. Dad asked questions, some sensible and some absolutely not."},{"p":"By the time we turned onto our street, I had stopped wishing quite so hard that the afternoon had gone differently."},{"p":"Not completely. I still wanted to fly."},{"p":"I just didn’t want to pretend the wind had agreed to my plan."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seventeen — The Flag That Lied","blocks":[{"p":"On Saturday morning, Beckett arrived at the academy carrying a fishing rod."},{"p":"Not a complete fishing rod. Just the top half, with a red strip of plastic grocery bag tied to the tip."},{"p":"Rafe stared at it. “If you catch a Cessna, you have to throw it back.”"},{"p":"“It’s a wind indicator,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“It’s a stick wearing trash.”"},{"p":"“It’s a prototype.”"},{"p":"“It’s trash with ambition.”"},{"p":"I was laughing when Mara came out of the office with a clipboard and told us to bring the prototype around back."},{"p":"The morning was warm enough that the pavement smelled like it had been left in an oven. A row of small airplanes sat tied down beyond the fence. The windsock near the runway hung at an angle, orange and white stripes twitching. Beckett’s plastic flag snapped in the breeze."},{"p":"“Look,” he said. “More sensitive than the sock.”"},{"p":"“More dramatic than the sock,” Imani said."},{"p":"Mara had picked three observation spots on the public side of the airport fence. Even Beckett’s flag wasn’t worth wandering onto a taxiway."},{"p":"At the first spot, beside the classroom, the flag pointed steadily away from the wind. At the second, near the hangar corner, it whipped around so wildly that Beckett had to hold the rod with both hands."},{"p":"“The wind keeps changing direction,” he said."},{"p":"“Or the building is changing what you feel,” Imani answered."},{"p":"He opened his mouth, then closed it."},{"p":"Mara let us stand there for a minute. A delivery van rolled through the parking lot, sending grit skittering across the concrete. The plastic strip twisted around the rod, freed itself, and twisted again."},{"p":"“What does this tell you?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“That grocery bags are annoying,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“That the wind near the corner isn’t steady,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. What doesn’t it tell you?”"},{"p":"I glanced toward the runway. “What the wind is doing over there.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"At the third spot, in an open patch of grass, Beckett’s flag settled down. It pointed almost the same way as the windsock. Imani wrote the observation in our notebook, including the time and where we’d stood. Beckett added a sketch of the hangar and drew a little face on the flag."},{"p":"“You’ve made it look angry,” I said."},{"p":"“It has been through a lot.”"},{"p":"Back inside, Mara pulled up a recent airport weather report and placed it beside our notes. The reported wind and our observations were not identical. I started to tell her the report was wrong, but stopped myself."},{"p":"“The report is from a specific observation,” I said instead. “We were standing in different places.”"},{"p":"“Right. That report tells us what was observed at one place and time. The wind by this building can be different.”"},{"p":"“Then how do pilots know what they’re going to get?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“They gather the best information available, pay attention to changes, and keep evaluating. They also understand the limits of what any one piece of information can tell them.”"},{"p":"Sensible. Inconvenient. I preferred answers I could underline."},{"p":"Mara divided us into pairs for a scenario. One pair would make a plan for a training flight. The other would introduce new information halfway through, as if conditions had changed. She handed Imani and me a sheet showing a fictional training-airport layout, a forecast, and a note about a nearby weather observation station. Beckett and Rafe received the change cards."},{"p":"“Don’t look at ours,” Rafe warned."},{"p":"“I wasn’t.”"},{"p":"“You were leaning.”"},{"p":"“I have a neck.”"},{"p":"“Suspicious.”"},{"p":"Imani drew two columns: what we knew and what we needed to know. I wanted to skip straight to choosing a runway, but she put her pencil across the page."},{"p":"“Start with the question,” she said."},{"p":"“Which runway?”"},{"p":"“Before that.”"},{"p":"I thought about it. “Whether the lesson makes sense today.”"},{"p":"She lifted the pencil. “Now we’re talking.”"},{"p":"We listed the wind report, possible gusts, the instructor’s experience, and the purpose of the lesson. We added a reminder to check current information before departure and again as appropriate. I was beginning to enjoy the exercise when Beckett slid his first card across the table."},{"p":"NEW REPORT: WIND SHIFTED. GUSTS INCREASING."},{"p":"“Of course they are,” I said."},{"p":"Rafe leaned back. “The weather does not care about your feelings.”"},{"p":"“Neither do you.”"},{"p":"“Correct.”"},{"p":"Imani didn’t laugh. She was reading the numbers, comparing the wind direction with the runway. “We should reassess,” she said."},{"p":"“We can still use the other runway,” I said."},{"p":"“Maybe. But what about the gusts?”"},{"p":"I reached for the pencil. “We could calculate the crosswind component.”"},{"p":"“And then?”"},{"p":"I looked at the sheet. In the past, I’d treated a calculation like a door. Get the number, walk through, done. But a number couldn’t tell us how a student would handle a gust, or whether the lesson would still teach what Mara wanted it to teach."},{"p":"“Then we’d talk it through with Mara,” I said. “Maybe we’d do a different lesson.”"},{"p":"Beckett slid over the second card."},{"p":"STUDENT SAYS: “WE’VE ALREADY WAITED TWO WEEKS. CAN’T WE JUST TRY?”"},{"p":"“That student sounds familiar,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“It’s a fictional student,” I said."},{"p":"“Fictional student with your handwriting?”"},{"p":"The card was written in Beckett’s square, cramped letters. I threw an eraser at Rafe. It bounced off his shoulder and landed under a chair."},{"p":"Mara looked up from the next table. “Anything airborne over there?”"},{"p":"“Only office supplies,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Let’s keep them grounded.”"},{"p":"When it was our turn to deliver the change cards, I tried to make ours difficult. I gave Beckett and Rafe a runway closure and a forecast that looked better at a different airport. Rafe immediately declared he was flying to the beach for ice cream."},{"p":"“That’s not one of your choices,” Mara said."},{"p":"“It should be.”"},{"p":"Beckett, surprisingly, didn’t argue. He wrote WAIT across the top of his worksheet."},{"p":"“Really?” I asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“You don’t even want to check the other airport?”"},{"p":"“We can check it. But the point of our lesson is to practice here, with Mara. If we drive to a different airport just to get in the air, we’re changing the whole plan.”"},{"p":"He said it like the answer was obvious. I hated how surprised I was."},{"p":"At lunch we sat on the low wall outside the classroom, eating sandwiches from crinkling paper bags. Beckett had peanut butter and banana. Rafe had something with enough hot sauce to make his eyes water. Imani had brought sliced apples and refused to trade them for anything."},{"p":"Beckett balanced the fishing-rod tip against his knee. The plastic flag fluttered in a tiny breeze that barely reached us."},{"p":"“My dad used to take me fishing,” he said."},{"p":"I looked at him. He hadn’t mentioned his dad much lately."},{"p":"“That’s where you got it?”"},{"p":"“Yeah. Broke the bottom half last summer.” He ran a thumb over the taped end. “I kept the rest.”"},{"p":"I almost asked why he’d kept a broken rod. Instead, I said, “It made a pretty good flag.”"},{"p":"“Pretty good?”"},{"p":"“It did attempt to strangle itself on a hangar.”"},{"p":"“Experimental feature.”"},{"p":"We ate for a while. A small airplane taxied past beyond the fence, its propeller a blurry circle. Beckett watched it until it disappeared behind the fuel pumps."},{"p":"“I didn’t like your joke in the group chat,” he said, without looking at me."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“I’m not saying we have to talk about it every week.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"“Just don’t make me the joke when you don’t know what’s going on.”"},{"p":"I picked a piece of crust off my sandwich. “I won’t.”"},{"p":"He nodded. Rafe, oblivious, was trying to convince Imani that hot sauce counted as a vegetable."},{"p":"But when we got up to go inside, Beckett handed me the flag to carry."},{"p":"I took it carefully. The plastic rattled in the breeze, pointing toward something I couldn’t see."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eighteen — A Different Kind of Landing","blocks":[{"p":"The following Tuesday, Mara put me in the right seat for the first ten minutes of our briefing."},{"p":"Not the airplane’s right seat. The right-hand chair at the classroom table, which was missing one rubber foot and rocked whenever I moved."},{"p":"“Your first assignment,” she said, “is to keep that chair from taking off.”"},{"p":"I wedged a folded index card beneath the leg. “Aircraft secured.”"},{"p":"“Excellent. Now tell me what we are not practicing today.”"},{"p":"“Crosswind landings,” I said."},{"p":"“Correct. If the weather cooperates, you’ll watch me handle the approach and landing. In the practice area, you’ll work on holding a ground track while I supervise.”"},{"p":"I looked at the airport diagram and tried not to seem disappointed. I’d been imagining a dramatic approach with the runway tilted under the nose and Mara calmly explaining everything while I watched. The practice area sounded less exciting."},{"p":"Unfortunately, Mara had never promised me an exciting lesson."},{"p":"We checked the weather together. The wind was blowing across the runway at a manageable angle for Mara, with no alarming change in the latest observations. She talked through the aircraft, the route, the training objectives, and what we’d do if the conditions shifted. We completed the inspection and checklists, and I kept my hands where they belonged unless she asked me to help."},{"p":"The cabin smelled faintly of warm plastic and old upholstery. Sunlight flashed off the wing strut. Once the engine was running, the familiar vibration came up through the soles of my shoes, and my stomach did its familiar nervous flip."},{"p":"Mara handled the taxi and takeoff. I watched the runway edge slide by and tried to connect the feeling of motion with the wind we’d discussed inside. Once we were at a safe altitude in the practice area and she’d cleared the area, she gave me the controls."},{"p":"“You have the flight controls.”"},{"p":"“I have the flight controls.”"},{"p":"“See that long road ahead, just left of the pond?”"},{"p":"I found it. From up here it looked like a gray shoelace pulled across green fields."},{"p":"“Let’s use it as a ground reference. We’re not following the road at road height, and we won’t maneuver over anything in particular. I want you to notice whether our track across the ground stays parallel to it.”"},{"p":"I nodded, adjusted my grip, and stared over the nose."},{"p":"For a few seconds everything looked easy. The airplane was pointed where I wanted to go. The road ran straight ahead. I felt like I could finally see what Mara had been trying to show me for months."},{"p":"Then the road began slipping sideways beneath us."},{"p":"“I’m drifting,” I said."},{"p":"“What can you do?”"},{"p":"“Adjust the heading into the wind.”"},{"p":"“Make a small change. Keep your eyes outside, and check your altitude and coordination.”"},{"p":"I eased the yoke and used the rudder as Mara had taught me, trying not to shove anything around. The nose moved. The road kept sliding for another few seconds, then slowed."},{"p":"“Now what?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“Wait and see.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"I waited. I hated waiting. The road was still not exactly where I wanted it. I made another correction before I could stop myself."},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“What do you know?”"},{"p":"“That I made it worse.”"},{"p":"“Then fix it calmly.”"},{"p":"I looked outside again and made a smaller correction. My altitude had wandered too, so I corrected that without trying to do everything in one movement. The airplane settled into a track that looked almost parallel to the road."},{"p":"“Better,” Mara said."},{"p":"It was the kind of better that didn’t make fireworks go off. It just made the next thirty seconds easier."},{"p":"We tried another reference line. The wind wasn’t constant, and I still needed reminders to look at the instruments without burying my face in them. Once, I mistook a slight change in the road’s direction for a change in our drift. Mara pointed out the bend before I made a huge correction."},{"p":"“Roads aren’t flight instruments,” she said."},{"p":"“Some of them aren’t even straight.”"},{"p":"“An excellent discovery.”"},{"p":"After a while, she took the controls and let me rest my hands on my knees. My fingers were damp."},{"p":"“What’s different from your first lessons?” she asked."},{"p":"“I know when I’m doing it wrong faster.”"},{"p":"She considered that. “That’s part of learning.”"},{"p":"“Not the part I was hoping for.”"},{"p":"“What part were you hoping for?”"},{"p":"“The part where I stop doing it wrong.”"},{"p":"She laughed. “You may be waiting a while for that one. Even experienced pilots keep learning and correcting. The goal is to notice what matters and respond appropriately.”"},{"p":"We turned toward the airport. I recognized a water tower we’d used as a landmark before, then lost it behind the wing and found it again. Mara briefed the approach and told me what she wanted me to observe. She would fly the landing; I would keep my hands and feet clear unless instructed otherwise."},{"p":"As we approached, the runway appeared slightly off the airplane’s nose. The picture was strange. I knew from our classroom models why an airplane might need a wind-correction angle, but seeing the pavement ahead while the nose pointed a little to one side made my brain insist something was wrong."},{"p":"“Are we lined up?” I asked."},{"p":"“We’re tracking toward the runway,” Mara said. “Watch what I do about the drift and our alignment as we get closer.”"},{"p":"I watched. The runway grew wider. The wind shifted enough that Mara made another adjustment. Her movements were smooth but not invisible; she was working, checking, and responding. Near touchdown she used the appropriate control inputs to align the airplane with the runway and prevent sideways drift."},{"p":"We landed without drama. The wheels touched, the airplane rolled straight, and Mara kept controlling it as we slowed. I had been holding my breath without noticing."},{"p":"“That’s it?” I said once we were clear of the runway and finished the appropriate checks."},{"p":"“That’s what?”"},{"p":"“The landing. I thought it would feel more—” I waved a hand, searching for a word. “Sideways.”"},{"p":"“Sometimes it does. Conditions vary. But we don’t aim for excitement.”"},{"p":"“Good thing, because I was ready to give it five stars.”"},{"p":"“Save the review until we’re parked.”"},{"p":"Back in the classroom, Mara asked me to write three things I’d noticed and one question I still had. I wrote that the airplane’s nose hadn’t always pointed exactly along its path, that the wind correction changed, and that Mara continued using the controls after the wheels touched."},{"p":"For my question, I wrote: HOW DO YOU KNOW WHEN TO GO AROUND?"},{"p":"Mara read it and drew a small check mark beside it."},{"p":"“Good question for our next briefing,” she said. “We can discuss stabilized approaches, unexpected changes, and why going around is a normal option. You don’t need to master the landing today.”"},{"p":"I folded the paper into my notebook. Outside, Beckett and Rafe were arguing over whether the fishing-rod flag needed a name. Imani had already rejected *Sir Flaps-a-Lot* and *Captain Trashwind*."},{"p":"“Windy McWindface,” I suggested."},{"p":"“Absolutely not,” Imani said."},{"p":"Rafe pointed at me. “That’s the winner.”"},{"p":"Beckett shook his head, but he was smiling."},{"p":"We stood outside a little longer than we needed to, watching the windsock twitch and settle. I tried to imagine all the air moving over the buildings, across the runway, and out toward the fields we’d just flown above. It wasn’t one neat arrow on a board. It was a thousand small changes, most of which I couldn’t see."},{"p":"I watched the windsock settle, then twitch again. This time, I waited to see what it would do next."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nineteen — The Second Choice","blocks":[{"p":"By Thursday, I had asked Mara my go-around question three different ways."},{"p":"The first was in my notebook. The second was in an email titled IMPORTANT QUESTION, which, in fairness, could have been the subject line of every email I’d ever sent Mara. The third was in person, while she was trying to unlock the classroom door with a cardboard drink tray tucked under one arm."},{"p":"“Lincoln,” she said, “if you hold the door, I can answer before the coffee gets cold.”"},{"p":"I grabbed the handle. “Deal.”"},{"p":"“A pilot goes around when continuing an approach or landing isn’t the safest choice. That can happen for a lot of reasons.”"},{"p":"“But when do you decide?”"},{"p":"“As soon as you recognize you need to. You don’t wait until the airplane is in trouble just to prove you can land it.”"},{"p":"I followed her inside. “What if you’ve already announced you’re landing?”"},{"p":"“At our airport, we make a traffic advisory when workload permits, so nearby pilots know what we’re doing. At a towered airport, we communicate with the controller. Either way, flying the airplane comes first.”"},{"p":"“What if everyone else is landing?”"},{"p":"She set the drinks on the table and looked at me. “Are you flying everyone else’s airplane?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Then their decisions don’t make yours for you.”"},{"p":"I wanted to argue that I’d only been asking a question. Unfortunately, she’d answered it."},{"p":"The others arrived a few minutes later. Rafe had a cardboard box balanced on his head, which he claimed was an experimental helmet. Imani said the experiment had already failed. Beckett carried the wind flag we’d been using for outdoor exercises, its bright fabric wrapped around the fishing rod."},{"p":"“Why is that here?” I asked."},{"p":"“Because it lives here now,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Does Mara know?”"},{"p":"“Mara has chosen not to ask questions.”"},{"p":"Mara looked up from the whiteboard. “I heard that.”"},{"p":"“See?” Beckett whispered. “Not a question.”"},{"p":"We started with photographs of approaches. One showed a runway disappearing beneath a low gray cloud bank. Another showed an airplane high above a runway with much less pavement left ahead than I expected. A third was a perfectly ordinary sunny-day picture."},{"p":"“Which one needs a go-around?” Mara asked."},{"p":"Rafe pointed at the cloudy one. “That one looks terrible.”"},{"p":"“It might,” Mara said. “What information would you need?”"},{"p":"We listed weather, visibility, runway conditions, airplane position, speed, and whether the approach was stable. Imani added that a photograph didn’t tell us what the airplane was doing a few seconds before or after it was taken."},{"p":"“Exactly,” Mara said. “A snapshot can help us notice things, but it isn’t the whole flight.”"},{"p":"I picked the high airplane. “If you’re too high, you shouldn’t force it down just because the runway’s there.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"“What about the sunny one?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"Mara smiled a little. “A deer could run onto the runway. A vehicle could be where it shouldn’t. A gust could change things. A pilot might notice something that makes the approach unsafe. Blue sky doesn’t promise a landing.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “Could the deer be wearing a headset?”"},{"p":"“Only if it has completed the required paperwork,” Mara said."},{"p":"She moved the pictures aside and drew a simple rectangle for a runway. Around it she marked a few places where a pilot might recognize an approach wasn’t going as planned. She said there weren’t magic spots on the ground where a go-around suddenly became allowed. A go-around could be necessary at different stages, and the exact procedure depended on the aircraft and circumstances."},{"p":"“We’re talking about decisions today,” she said, tapping the board. “Not teaching you to perform a go-around on your own. When you train for that maneuver, you’ll learn and practice the correct procedure with an instructor.”"},{"p":"That seemed fair, even if I wanted to know every step immediately."},{"p":"We split into pairs for a scenario exercise. Imani and I got a card that said our imaginary airplane was approaching an airport after a short training flight. The wind had changed, and the airplane wasn’t lined up the way the pilot wanted. The card asked what questions we should ask before deciding whether to continue."},{"p":"“How much has the wind changed?” I said."},{"p":"Imani wrote it down."},{"p":"“Are we drifting? Are we at the right speed? Are we stable?”"},{"p":"She added those, then paused. “Do we have enough runway left to make a normal landing?”"},{"p":"“Good one.”"},{"p":"“And what does our instructor think?”"},{"p":"I stared at her. “We’re pretending to be the pilot.”"},{"p":"“We’re fourteen,” she said. “Our imaginary airplane should probably have an instructor.”"},{"p":"I laughed. “Fine. Put that first.”"},{"p":"Across the room, Beckett and Rafe were arguing about a card involving another airplane still on the runway. Rafe insisted the best solution was for the other airplane to hurry up. Beckett asked whether Rafe planned to lean out the window and yell."},{"p":"“I could,” Rafe said. “I’ve got a strong voice.”"},{"p":"Mara took their card away and handed them a new one."},{"p":"After class, I helped stack the chairs while Mara collected the worksheets. Through the window I could see an airplane taxiing toward the runway. Its wings rocked slightly over a bump in the pavement."},{"p":"“I think I get it,” I told her."},{"p":"“What part?”"},{"p":"“Going around isn’t losing.”"},{"p":"She put the papers in a folder. “That’s one way to describe it.”"},{"p":"“But you can still feel like you lost.”"},{"p":"“Of course. You can be disappointed and make the right decision anyway.”"},{"p":"I thought about our canceled flights, the boat at Houghton Lake that stayed tied to the dock, and the afternoons I’d spent wishing the wind would do something different just because I’d made plans."},{"p":"“I don’t think I’m very good at that part yet,” I said."},{"p":"“Which part?”"},{"p":"“Being disappointed without acting like everyone else caused it.”"},{"p":"Mara glanced toward the door, where Beckett was attempting to fit the fishing-rod flag into his backpack. “You’ve got opportunities to practice.”"},{"p":"I went outside and found Beckett with the rod stuck through both sleeves of his jacket."},{"p":"“Need help?” I asked."},{"p":"“No. This is exactly how I intended to carry it.”"},{"p":"“Looks aerodynamic.”"},{"p":"“Thank you.”"},{"p":"I held the backpack while he worked the rod free. We walked toward the parking lot with Rafe and Imani, the four of us talking over one another about whether a windsock would make a good Halloween costume."},{"p":"I kept what I’d told Mara to myself. Beckett was still wrestling the flag into his backpack. Helping him seemed like a better start than making an announcement."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty — The Runway Beyond the Window","blocks":[{"p":"Saturday’s wind was supposed to be mild."},{"p":"I checked the forecast Friday evening, then again before bed, then once more Saturday morning while eating cereal. Dad watched me over the top of his book."},{"p":"“Are you trying to wear the weather out?” he asked."},{"p":"“It changed since last night.”"},{"p":"“Did you ask it politely not to?”"},{"p":"“It’s a forecast. It’s allowed to change.”"},{"p":"“So are plans,” he said, and turned a page."},{"p":"I made a face into my cereal. Dad didn’t even look up."},{"p":"At Hampton Roads Executive Airport, the air was cool enough that I wished I’d brought a sweatshirt. The sky had thin clouds that looked as if someone had dragged a comb across wet paint. A breeze tugged at the edge of Mara’s clipboard while we reviewed the day’s lesson."},{"p":"We were scheduled for another dual flight. She planned to demonstrate an approach and, if conditions remained suitable, a go-around. My part would be to observe, help with the normal checklist tasks she assigned, and practice holding a ground track in the training area. She reminded me that any maneuvering near the runway was hers to fly."},{"p":"“You seem quiet,” she said."},{"p":"“I’m trying not to talk myself into being nervous.”"},{"p":"“You can be nervous. Just don’t let it make decisions without you.”"},{"p":"I wrote that down on the back of my lunch receipt. Inspirational words, questionable stationery."},{"p":"During the preflight inspection, I worked through the checklist with her, checking what she asked me to check and pointing out anything I wasn’t sure about. The airplane looked ordinary. It was the same white-and-blue Cessna, the same scuffed step below the door, the same little scratch on the plastic trim near the passenger seat. Recognizing the scratches helped settle my nerves."},{"p":"Once we were airborne, Mara gave me the controls in the practice area. We picked a line of trees as a ground reference. The wind pushed us sideways more than I’d expected from the view out the window."},{"p":"“What do you see?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“We’re drifting to the right of the line.”"},{"p":"“What would you like to change?”"},{"p":"“Our heading, a little into the wind.”"},{"p":"“Show me.”"},{"p":"I made a small, coordinated turn. Then I waited, which was still the hardest part. The trees stayed almost parallel beneath us."},{"p":"“That works better,” I said."},{"p":"“What works better?”"},{"p":"“Not chasing it every second.”"},{"p":"“Good observation.”"},{"p":"The airplane bumped through a patch of uneven air. My hand tightened on the yoke, but I loosened it before I made the enormous correction my thirteen-year-old self would have tried."},{"p":"We practiced a little longer. I lost altitude once because I was too busy watching the trees. Another time I forgot to scan the instruments until Mara reminded me. I wasn’t brilliant. But I caught a few mistakes before Mara had to point them out."},{"p":"Then Mara took the controls and we headed back toward the airport."},{"p":"The radio was busier than it had been earlier. Mara announced our position and listened for other traffic. The airport was operating normally, but a gust had arrived with a slightly different direction from the one we’d briefed."},{"p":"“We’ll reassess before the approach,” she said."},{"p":"I watched the windsock near the field as we got closer. It wasn’t doing anything dramatic. It lifted, sagged, and lifted again."},{"p":"“Is that too much?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not necessarily. We consider the full conditions, the airplane, and the pilot. I’m going to demonstrate the approach if it’s appropriate. Watch the runway and our path toward it.”"},{"p":"She briefed what she planned to do, including the possibility of a go-around. Her voice sounded exactly the way it had during the preflight inspection: calm, precise, not especially interested in making the afternoon memorable."},{"p":"The runway appeared ahead, a dark strip with white markings. I could see a truck moving along a road beyond the airport fence. The airplane’s nose pointed a little into the wind as Mara corrected for drift."},{"p":"“We aren’t pointed exactly down the runway,” I said."},{"p":"“But look at where we’re traveling.”"},{"p":"I looked. The runway stayed in front of us."},{"p":"As we descended, a gust shifted the airplane. Mara corrected, then watched how it responded. A moment later another change came through. The runway moved in the windshield more than I liked."},{"p":"“That didn’t look right,” I said."},{"p":"“I agree.”"},{"p":"There was no alarm in her voice, but I heard the decision before she said it."},{"p":"“We’re going around.”"},{"p":"Mara added power and began the airplane’s go-around procedure. As we climbed, she made the appropriate traffic announcement. I kept my hands and feet clear, just as we’d briefed. The engine grew louder. The runway stopped getting bigger beneath us and began slipping away as the airplane climbed."},{"p":"My stomach dropped even though we were going up."},{"p":"For a second I felt embarrassed, which made absolutely no sense. I hadn’t been flying. I hadn’t even been the one to decide. Still, part of me wanted to explain to the airport, the truck driver, and every person who might have been looking up that we could have landed if we’d wanted to."},{"p":"Then I remembered Mara’s question from Thursday: *Are you flying everyone else’s airplane?*"},{"p":"No. We were flying ours."},{"p":"Mara continued the climb, watching for traffic as she rejoined the traffic pattern. She didn’t deliver a speech or ask me to admire her decision. She had work to do."},{"p":"Once things were settled, she glanced at me. “You okay?”"},{"p":"“Yeah.” I swallowed. “That happened fast.”"},{"p":"“Sometimes the decision needs to be made quickly. That’s why we plan for the possibility before we need it.”"},{"p":"“Were we in danger?”"},{"p":"“We recognized that the approach wasn’t developing the way I wanted. Going around gave us another option instead of forcing the landing.”"},{"p":"I looked back toward the airport. The runway was smaller now, and the airplane felt steady again."},{"p":"“So we try again?” I asked."},{"p":"“We’ll evaluate the conditions and decide whether another approach is appropriate or whether we should use another option.”"},{"p":"I waited for her to tell me what the right answer would be. She didn’t. She checked the conditions, considered the situation, and talked me through the factors without turning it into a quiz."},{"p":"After reassessing the wind and traffic, Mara flew another approach and landed. It was a normal landing, not the heroic ending my imagination would have preferred. After we cleared the runway, Mara finished the checks and taxied toward the parking area."},{"p":"My legs felt strangely tired when I climbed out."},{"p":"Inside, Beckett was waiting with a bottle of water and a look that meant he had already heard something from Rafe."},{"p":"“Did you really go around?” he asked."},{"p":"“Yep.”"},{"p":"“What was it like?”"},{"p":"“Loud,” I said. “And then not landing.”"},{"p":"“That’s an awful review.”"},{"p":"“Two stars. Great view, no arrival.”"},{"p":"He laughed and handed me the water."},{"p":"Mara came through the door carrying her flight bag. “For the record, I recommend five stars for a safe decision.”"},{"p":"“Customer service is very defensive here,” Beckett said."},{"p":"Rafe wanted the entire story, especially whether I had been scared. I told him the truth: a little, mostly because I hadn’t expected it. Imani asked what I’d noticed before Mara made the decision. I described the runway shifting in the windshield and the way the wind had changed near the ground."},{"p":"“So you knew what was happening?” she asked."},{"p":"“Some of it. Mara knew a lot more.”"},{"p":"That answer would have bothered me last year. Now it seemed like the most useful thing I could say."},{"p":"When Dad arrived, I climbed into the car and told him about the go-around before he’d even started the engine."},{"p":"“Did you land eventually?” he asked."},{"p":"“Yeah. But that’s not the interesting part.”"},{"p":"“All right,” he said. “What’s the interesting part?”"},{"p":"I watched a small airplane cross the sky beyond the windshield. From the ground, it looked as if it were flying perfectly straight. I wondered how many tiny corrections the pilot was making that nobody could see."},{"p":"“We had a plan,” I said. “And then we used a different one.”"},{"p":"Dad put on his seat belt. “That sounds suspiciously like something you’ve been telling me for months.”"},{"p":"“Don’t get used to it.”"},{"p":"He grinned and backed out of the parking space."},{"p":"On the ride home, I opened my notebook and drew a small runway. Beside it I wrote one sentence, then underlined it twice:"},{"p":"*You don’t owe the runway a landing.*"}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-One — The Forecast That Changed","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday morning, the weather forecast said Saturday would be sunny."},{"p":"By Tuesday, it said partly cloudy."},{"p":"By Wednesday, a little lightning bolt had appeared on the forecast. It looked much too cheerful for a threat to our weekend."},{"p":"“The forecast is playing a prank on you,” Dad said over breakfast."},{"p":"I slid my phone across the table. “It’s not funny.”"},{"p":"“I didn’t say it was a good prank.”"},{"p":"Saturday was the academy’s end-of-summer open house. Families could visit the classrooms, look at the training airplanes from behind the marked barriers, and watch students demonstrate what they’d learned. We weren’t putting on an air show. Mara had made that very clear, twice, after Rafe suggested we should have a smoke machine."},{"p":"“It’s an airport,” she’d said. “Not your birthday party.”"},{"p":"“My birthday party had better snacks,” Rafe had replied."},{"p":"I was supposed to help run the wind station with Imani and Beckett. We had a windsock model, a map of the field, and a board where visitors could guess which direction a paper airplane would drift if a fan blew across its path."},{"p":"I wanted our station to be the one everyone remembered."},{"p":"By Thursday, the forecast had changed again. A line of thunderstorms was possible in the afternoon. The morning looked better, but nobody could say exactly when the storms might arrive."},{"p":"I called Imani."},{"p":"“Do you think they’ll cancel?” I asked."},{"p":"“They might move the outdoor parts inside.”"},{"p":"“That ruins the whole point of a wind station.”"},{"p":"“Does it?”"},{"p":"“We need actual wind.”"},{"p":"“We have a fan.”"},{"p":"“That’s not the same.”"},{"p":"“Then we can explain why it’s not the same. That’s sort of our job.”"},{"p":"I hated how quickly Imani could make a good point."},{"p":"Friday afternoon, I got to the academy early. Beckett was already kneeling on the classroom floor with a roll of tape stuck around his wrist. He had built a miniature cardboard runway with white markings cut from printer paper. A tiny windsock stood beside it on a bent drinking straw."},{"p":"“Please tell me you didn’t steal that from a restaurant,” I said."},{"p":"“I stole it from my kitchen. Different jurisdiction.”"},{"p":"He flicked the windsock. It spun and then fell sideways."},{"p":"“It needs a better bearing,” he said."},{"p":"“It needs a better everything.”"},{"p":"He gave me a look. “Good morning to you, too.”"},{"p":"I set down my backpack. “Sorry. The weather’s making me crazy.”"},{"p":"“The weather doesn’t know you exist.”"},{"p":"“That’s the problem.”"},{"p":"For half an hour, every fix created a new problem. The paper sleeve was too heavy. The straw leaned. The cardboard runway warped because Beckett had used too much glue."},{"p":"Finally Imani arrived, watched us struggle for about ten seconds, and said, “Why don’t you use a pin?”"},{"p":"Beckett stared at the table."},{"p":"I stared at Beckett."},{"p":"“I have pins,” he said."},{"p":"“Then why didn’t you—” I began."},{"p":"“Because,” he said, “I wanted to suffer.”"},{"p":"With a pin for the pivot and a lighter paper sleeve, the little windsock turned in the breeze from the fan. It looked like airport equipment for a very determined mouse. But it turned."},{"p":"Mara came in carrying a stack of visitor handouts. She watched the windsock turn, then studied our display board."},{"p":"“What are you telling people?” she asked."},{"p":"“That wind can change an airplane’s path over the ground,” I said. “Even when the nose points in a different direction.”"},{"p":"“Good start. What else?”"},{"p":"Imani pointed at the chart. “That a wind report is a useful observation, but it doesn’t guarantee the wind will stay the same everywhere or every second.”"},{"p":"“And that you shouldn’t trust a straw,” Beckett added."},{"p":"Mara smiled. “I’d put that last one in smaller print.”"},{"p":"Then she set the handouts on a desk and turned serious."},{"p":"“We’re watching tomorrow’s forecast with the airport staff,” Mara said. “We’ll decide about outdoor activities in the morning and keep checking throughout the event. If conditions turn unsafe, we move inside or stop. No demonstration is worth taking chances for a crowd.”"},{"p":"“What if people are disappointed?” I asked."},{"p":"“They can be disappointed safely.”"},{"p":"I thought about our go-around. How normal Mara had made it seem. How strange it had felt to climb away from a runway we were already so close to using."},{"p":"“What if the weather is fine when we start?” I asked."},{"p":"“Then we keep checking it. A good decision at nine o’clock isn’t permission to stop paying attention at noon.”"},{"p":"That night I checked the forecast again. And again. Dad caught me at the kitchen counter, refreshing the page as if I could persuade the clouds to move by looking at them hard enough."},{"p":"“Come help me carry these boxes,” he said."},{"p":"He was sorting books for a school donation drive. Three cartons stood by the front door. A fourth had split along the bottom. A paperback about dinosaurs lay on the floor, face down, as if the dinosaurs were embarrassed."},{"p":"“Can it wait?”"},{"p":"“Not unless you’ve invented a way to make books carry themselves.”"},{"p":"I put my phone away. We carried the boxes to the car. The evening air smelled like wet grass, even though it hadn’t rained yet. Across the street, our neighbor’s wind chime knocked against the porch rail."},{"p":"Dad shut the trunk. “You know, I used to hate the day before a school event.”"},{"p":"“Because of the weather?”"},{"p":"“Because I imagined every possible thing that could go wrong. Usually the thing that went wrong was something I hadn’t imagined at all.”"},{"p":"“That’s not encouraging.”"},{"p":"“No,” he admitted. “But it helped me stop pretending I could plan every detail.”"},{"p":"I leaned against the car. “I just want tomorrow to be good.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“We worked really hard.”"},{"p":"“I know that, too.”"},{"p":"He stayed beside me while the wind chime clinked again."},{"p":"On Saturday, the sky was pale blue when we reached the academy. By ten, families were moving between the classrooms and the marked viewing area. A little boy in a dinosaur shirt asked whether our paper airplane could fly to the moon. Beckett told him only if the moon moved closer and the airplane developed an engine."},{"p":"The boy considered that. “My uncle has a truck.”"},{"p":"“That could help,” Beckett said."},{"p":"Imani explained the fan experiment to a girl about our age who asked why the airplane didn’t simply turn itself into the wind. Rafe handled the crowd, which mostly meant talking enthusiastically enough that nobody noticed when we had to reset the cardboard runway."},{"p":"For an hour, everything worked."},{"p":"Then the light changed."},{"p":"It wasn’t dark, exactly. The sunshine flattened. Beyond the hangars, the clouds had thickened. A breeze pushed across the viewing area, carrying the smell of rain."},{"p":"I looked at the windsock on the field. It lifted higher and shifted direction."},{"p":"“That wasn’t what it was doing earlier,” I said."},{"p":"Imani followed my gaze. “Nope.”"},{"p":"The adults were responsible for the event, but I could still tell Mara what I saw."},{"p":"I found her near the classroom door. “The wind’s picked up, and those clouds are getting closer.”"},{"p":"“I’ve seen them,” she said. “Thank you for saying something. We’re moving everyone inside now.”"},{"p":"A few visitors groaned when the announcement came over the speaker. One man asked whether he could just take a quick photograph beside the airplane before they closed the viewing area."},{"p":"Mara shook her head. “Not outside the designated area, sir. We’re bringing everyone in.”"},{"p":"He wasn’t pleased. Neither was the little boy in the dinosaur shirt, who had apparently been promised a second chance to send his airplane to the moon."},{"p":"I helped Beckett carry our display through the door. A gust caught the cardboard runway and nearly folded it in half."},{"p":"“Careful!” he shouted."},{"p":"“I’ve got it!”"},{"p":"I didn’t. The runway slipped free and skidded across the floor."},{"p":"Beckett chased it, grabbed it before it reached a trash can, and held it up. One corner was bent."},{"p":"For a second I wanted to blame the wind. Or the tape. Or Beckett’s glue."},{"p":"“I dropped it,” I said instead."},{"p":"“I noticed.”"},{"p":"“We can fix it.”"},{"p":"“Yeah,” he said. “We can.”"},{"p":"We moved the station to a classroom table. The wind outside rattled the door, and rain began ticking against the windows. Our cardboard runway looked worse under fluorescent lights, but people still came over. They still asked questions. The dinosaur boy came back and launched his paper airplane so hard it hit Rafe in the shoulder."},{"p":"“Moon mission failed,” Rafe announced."},{"p":"The boy laughed so hard he had to sit down."},{"p":"I glanced out the window. The real runway was gray under the rain. Nothing about the day looked the way I’d pictured it."},{"p":"I grinned when the dinosaur boy demanded another launch."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Two — What We Kept","blocks":[{"p":"The rain stopped before the open house ended, but the outdoor displays stayed closed. Mara explained that a break in the rain didn’t automatically mean the conditions were suitable, and the event continued inside."},{"p":"By the time the last family left, the classroom smelled like damp jackets and the cheese crackers Rafe had spilled under a table."},{"p":"“Those are going to be there forever,” I said."},{"p":"“Then future students can learn archaeology,” he replied."},{"p":"Beckett sat cross-legged on the floor, inspecting the bent corner of our cardboard runway. Imani was collecting the visitor question cards. Some held questions, some held drawings, and one requested a flying dinosaur in very careful handwriting."},{"p":"“How many people came through?” I asked."},{"p":"“Forty-three stopped at our table,” Imani said. “I counted.”"},{"p":"“Of course you did.”"},{"p":"“Twenty-one tried the fan experiment. Nine asked about becoming pilots. And one asked whether airplanes have reverse gear.”"},{"p":"“Some aircraft can move backward on the ground,” Beckett said, looking up. “But it depends on the aircraft, and it’s not like putting a car in reverse.”"},{"p":"“Please don’t start,” Rafe said. “I’m running on two crackers and a juice box.”"},{"p":"Mara joined us carrying a trash bag. “You four did good work today.”"},{"p":"I waited for the part where she mentioned the broken runway."},{"p":"“Especially when the plan changed,” she added."},{"p":"“The runway got damaged,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s cardboard.”"},{"p":"“It was good cardboard.”"},{"p":"“Then you can repair it for the next event.”"},{"p":"I picked up a question card. A younger student had drawn a lopsided airplane with an enormous windsock beside it. Underneath, in crooked letters, were the words: WHY DOES WIND PUSH THINGS?"},{"p":"I showed it to Mara. “We didn’t answer this one.”"},{"p":"“What would you tell them?”"},{"p":"I started with the answer I would have given last summer. Something about air pressure and moving masses of air and forces. It sounded impressive in my head, which was usually a warning sign."},{"p":"“I’d tell them wind is moving air,” I said instead. “And moving air can push on things. How much it changes an airplane’s path depends on the wind and how the airplane is moving.”"},{"p":"“That’s a good explanation for a younger visitor,” Mara said. “You can build on it if they ask more.”"},{"p":"I slipped the card into my pocket."},{"p":"After cleanup, the four of us waited beneath the awning for our rides. Puddles shone on the pavement. The wind had settled, leaving the air thick and sticky."},{"p":"Rafe was explaining why the open house needed an official mascot next year."},{"p":"“A windsock,” he said. “With arms.”"},{"p":"“Windsocks don’t have arms,” Imani said."},{"p":"“That’s why ours would be special.”"},{"p":"“It would look like a haunted noodle,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“You’re just jealous you didn’t think of it.”"},{"p":"I laughed, then noticed Beckett looking down at his phone. His smile slipped. I knew that look."},{"p":"“Everything okay?” I asked."},{"p":"“Mom’s working late. My aunt can get me, but not for another hour.”"},{"p":"“Dad can probably take you,” I said."},{"p":"Beckett shook his head before I’d finished. “It’s fine.”"},{"p":"I remembered the last time we’d talked about rides. How I’d made it sound as if getting somewhere was just a matter of trying harder. How long it had taken me to understand why he’d been angry."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “Want to wait with us anyway?”"},{"p":"He looked at me. “Yeah.”"},{"p":"We stayed under the awning while Rafe explained his haunted-noodle costume. Imani threatened to put him inside an actual windsock."},{"p":"Dad arrived fifteen minutes later. He rolled down the passenger window."},{"p":"“Anybody need a ride?”"},{"p":"Beckett hesitated."},{"p":"“We’ve got room,” Dad said. “But only if your family says it’s okay.”"},{"p":"Beckett called his mother, then climbed into the back seat with his backpack on his knees. I sat beside him. Rafe had claimed the front seat even though his own ride was three minutes away. I decided not to ask."},{"p":"After Rafe left, Dad drove us toward Beckett’s neighborhood. The roads were still wet, and water sprayed from the tires of the cars ahead."},{"p":"“How’d the event go?” Dad asked."},{"p":"“We got rained out,” I said."},{"p":"“Partly,” Beckett corrected."},{"p":"“Our runway broke.”"},{"p":"“Also partly,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“And a child attacked Rafe with a paper airplane.”"},{"p":"“That part was a complete success.”"},{"p":"Dad glanced at us in the mirror. “Sounds memorable.”"},{"p":"I watched the rainwater slip along the edge of the window. “It was.”"},{"p":"When we reached Beckett’s street, he unbuckled his seat belt but didn’t open the door right away."},{"p":"“Hey,” he said. “Thanks for the ride.”"},{"p":"“Sure,” Dad said."},{"p":"Beckett looked at me. “And for not making it weird.”"},{"p":"“I make plenty of other things weird,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s true.”"},{"p":"He got out before I could come up with a better answer."},{"p":"That evening I spread my notebook and the visitor cards across the kitchen table. Mom was chopping vegetables for dinner. The knife made quick, regular taps against the cutting board."},{"p":"“How was your big day?” she asked."},{"p":"“Wet.”"},{"p":"“I gathered that from your shoes.”"},{"p":"I told her about the wind station, the little boy who wanted to reach the moon, and the change to the indoor plan. I told her about the runway getting bent. She listened while she scraped chopped carrots into a bowl."},{"p":"“You sound happy,” she said."},{"p":"“I guess I am.”"},{"p":"“Even though it didn’t go according to plan?”"},{"p":"“Especially that part, maybe. Not the broken runway. But we had to figure stuff out.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “That’s usually where the interesting stories are.”"},{"p":"I opened my notebook to the page where I’d written about the go-around. The sentence was still there, underlined twice: You don’t owe the runway a landing."},{"p":"Below it I made a list of things we’d changed at the open house. Moved indoors. Used the fan. Repaired the model. Answered different questions. Kept everyone out of the weather."},{"p":"The list didn’t look particularly exciting. It didn’t include a dramatic landing or a trophy or even a picture of an airplane in flight."},{"p":"But the little boy’s question card sat beside it, the airplane’s wings drawn so unevenly that one looked like a giant ear."},{"p":"I wrote an answer on the back, then crossed out *always*. Imani would have caught that word in about three seconds."},{"p":"Outside, the last of the rainwater dripped from the gutters. Dad called from the living room that dinner was almost ready."},{"p":"I put the card inside my notebook, where it wouldn’t get lost."},{"p":"Next summer I would be fifteen. There would be more lessons, more forecasts, more days when I wanted the sky to behave the way I’d planned."},{"p":"I turned back to the dinosaur drawing and added one more note for its pilot: *Bring a backup plan.*"},{"p":"I still didn’t like having my plans changed. I doubted I ever would."},{"p":"But I was starting to understand that changing a plan wasn’t the same as giving up on it."},{"p":"Sometimes it was how you got to keep going."}]},{"t":"Epilogue — The Flag at Third Base","blocks":[{"p":"The first Saturday in September, I lost my baseball cap to a wind gust before the game even started."},{"p":"The brim was permanently bent from living in my backpack, and the stitching was coming loose. Still, it was mine, and watching it cartwheel across the parking lot made me sprint after it with a glove on one hand and a half-eaten granola bar in the other."},{"p":"I caught it beneath somebody’s minivan."},{"p":"“Nice fielding,” Dad said when I emerged."},{"p":"“It was a ground ball.”"},{"p":"“Looked more like a pop fly to me.”"},{"p":"Mom leaned against the car, trying to keep her hair out of her face. “If the wind is this bad, are they even going to play?”"},{"p":"“Coach says yes,” I told her. “Unless the weather changes.”"},{"p":"Dad looked at Mom over the roof of the car. “Listen to that. Our son has discovered the word unless.”"},{"p":"“Don’t make it weird,” I said."},{"p":"He grinned. “Never.”"},{"p":"At the field, the little orange flag beyond third base snapped straight out, then sagged, then snapped again. I stood by the dugout and watched it while our pitcher warmed up. A few months earlier, I would have decided the flag told me everything about the wind. Now I noticed the trees beyond center field moving in a different rhythm. The gusts weren’t arriving everywhere at once."},{"p":"“Burgess,” Coach called. “You planning to marry that flag or take your position?”"},{"p":"“Position,” I said."},{"p":"Rafe had come to watch, mostly because he believed he could provide better commentary than the parents in the bleachers. He cupped his hands around his mouth as I jogged toward right field."},{"p":"“WIND ADVISORY FOR THE RIGHT FIELDER!”"},{"p":"“Rafe!” Coach barked."},{"p":"“That was my quiet voice!”"},{"p":"I was still laughing when the first batter stepped up."},{"p":"The game was close enough that nobody on our bench had time to relax. By the fourth inning we were down a run. In the fifth, a hard line drive went over the second baseman’s glove, and I chased it toward the fence, picked it up cleanly, and threw it to the cutoff man instead of trying a heroic throw all the way home. The runner stayed at second."},{"p":"I forgot about the throw until I reached the dugout."},{"p":"“Good decision,” Coach said."},{"p":"“I could’ve made home,” I said automatically."},{"p":"Coach raised an eyebrow."},{"p":"“Probably not,” I admitted."},{"p":"“Probably not.”"},{"p":"In the last inning, with two outs and the tying run on second, a batter sent a high fly toward the space between right and center. I heard the crack of the bat and started moving before I’d even found the ball."},{"p":"Then I saw it."},{"p":"It rose into the pale afternoon sky, a white dot against the clouds. The wind carried it farther toward the foul line than I expected. I changed direction, glanced once at the other outfielder to make sure we weren’t about to collide, and called, “Mine!”"},{"p":"For three steps, it looked like the ball would land behind me. I ran harder. Then the gust eased, and it seemed to hang almost motionless."},{"p":"I wanted to lunge."},{"p":"Instead, I kept my feet moving, got beneath it, and raised my glove."},{"p":"The ball struck the leather with a hollow smack."},{"p":"Three outs."},{"p":"Our team still lost by one run."},{"p":"I was disappointed about that, especially because Rafe announced that my catch deserved a dramatic replay and then performed one so badly that he fell over his own shoelaces. But I’d caught the ball, and I could still feel those three frantic steps when I’d nearly run past it."},{"p":"On the drive home, Mom asked whether I wanted to stop for ice cream."},{"p":"“We lost,” I said."},{"p":"“I know. Ice cream isn’t a trophy.”"},{"p":"“Then yes.”"},{"p":"Dad turned into the little shopping center by the highway. From the back seat, I could see a bank of gray clouds gathering in the west, taller than the ones we’d played under. They looked like heaps of laundry somebody had forgotten to fold."},{"p":"My phone buzzed. A message from Imani: *Did you survive the baseball wind tunnel?*"},{"p":"I typed: *Caught the last out. Lost the game.*"},{"p":"Beckett replied before Imani did: *Did the flag lie?*"},{"p":"*It told me part of the truth,* I wrote."},{"p":"Rafe sent a photograph of himself sprawled in the grass with the caption: *LOCAL COMMENTATOR INJURED IN HISTORIC REENACTMENT.*"},{"p":"I laughed so hard Dad asked what was happening."},{"p":"“Nothing,” I said. “Rafe’s being Rafe.”"},{"p":"Outside the ice cream shop, a small airplane passed above the rooftops, moving steadily east. From the ground I couldn’t know what the pilot was seeing or how the wind felt up there. I could only watch its path against the clouds."},{"p":"Next year I’d learn more about weather—more than a flag, more than a forecast icon on a phone. Mara had already warned us that there were plenty of ways to misunderstand the sky."},{"p":"I was looking forward to finding out what I didn’t know."},{"p":"But first I had to choose between chocolate and strawberry."},{"p":"That decision, at least, was easy."},{"p":"“Both,” I told Dad."},{"p":"He sighed. “I should’ve seen that coming.”"}]},{"t":"Ground School Notes — What the Wind Can Teach You","blocks":[{"p":"Lincoln is fourteen in this story. His lessons in the Cessna are supervised by Mara, a qualified flight instructor. These notes are for curious readers, not instructions for operating an airplane. Pilots use official training materials, aircraft-specific guidance, and instructor supervision to learn safe techniques."},{"h":"1. The Air Moves Even When You Can’t See It"},{"p":"Wind is air moving relative to the ground. You can see what it does to flags, tree branches, water, and blowing leaves, but you can’t always see the moving air itself. Those clues can also be misleading. A flag sheltered by a building may behave differently from one out in the open. Gusts may come and go."},{"p":"**Try this:** From a safe place, watch two wind indicators that are some distance apart, such as a tree and a flag. Do they move together? Record what you notice without deciding too quickly why it happens."},{"h":"2. Heading Is Not the Same as Ground Track"},{"p":"An airplane’s **heading** is the direction its nose points. Its **ground track** is the path it actually follows over Earth’s surface. A crosswind can push an airplane sideways, so a pilot may need to point the nose somewhat into the wind to travel along the intended ground track. This is called a **wind-correction angle**. The airplane’s nose may point a little away from its ground track even while the airplane follows the planned path."},{"p":"**Think about it:** Imagine swimming across a river with a current. Would pointing straight at the opposite bank necessarily bring you to the spot directly across from where you started? What would you have to consider?"},{"h":"3. Airspeed and Groundspeed Answer Different Questions"},{"p":"**Airspeed** describes an airplane’s motion relative to the surrounding air. **Groundspeed** describes how fast it moves across the ground. A headwind usually reduces groundspeed compared with the same flight in still air; a tailwind usually increases it. Neither number alone tells a pilot everything needed to fly safely."},{"p":"**Try this:** A pretend airplane travels through the air at 100 knots with a direct 20-knot headwind. Ignoring other complications, its groundspeed is about 80 knots. With a direct 20-knot tailwind, it’s about 120 knots. What changes when the wind comes from the side? Hint: you’ll need to think about direction as well as speed."},{"h":"4. What Is a Crosswind Component?"},{"p":"A runway has a direction, and wind has both a direction and a speed. The part of the wind blowing across the runway is the **crosswind component**. The part blowing along the runway is the **headwind or tailwind component**. Pilots can estimate these using training diagrams, charts, or calculations."},{"p":"A reported wind speed alone cannot tell a pilot whether a landing will be safe. Pilots must consider gusts, runway conditions, aircraft performance, published limitations, their own abilities, and their instructor’s judgment. A demonstrated crosswind value in an aircraft’s handbook records a tested condition; it is not automatically a legal limit or a guarantee of safe handling."},{"p":"**Try this:** Draw a runway as a vertical line. Add one wind arrow directly along it, one straight across it, and one at an angle. Which gives a full crosswind? Which gives no crosswind component?"},{"h":"5. Why Pilots Sometimes Point Away From the Runway’s Direction"},{"p":"On approach in a crosswind, an airplane may fly with its nose pointed somewhat into the wind while its path over the ground remains aligned with the runway. Pilots call this a **crab**. Near touchdown, the pilot must manage alignment and drift using appropriate, aircraft-specific techniques. A qualified instructor teaches how to do this safely."},{"p":"Reaching the runway is only part of landing safely; alignment, drift, and control matter too."},{"p":"**Think about it:** Why might a landing be unsafe even if the airplane is directly above the runway centerline?"},{"h":"6. A Go-Around Is a Normal Safety Choice"},{"p":"A **go-around** means discontinuing an approach to landing and transitioning to a climb using the proper procedure. Pilots may go around because the approach is unstable, the runway is obstructed, the wind changes, or something else makes landing unwise. It isn’t a punishment or proof of failure. Pilots train for go-arounds because changing a plan is part of flying safely."},{"p":"**Try this:** Think of a sport where a player can choose to stop, reset, or pass rather than force a risky move. How is that similar to a go-around? How is it different?"},{"h":"7. Weather Reports Have Limits"},{"p":"A **METAR** is a routine aviation weather observation for a particular airport, issued at a particular time. It can include wind direction and speed, visibility, cloud information, temperature, and other conditions. A **TAF** is an aviation forecast for an airport area over a stated period. Neither describes every patch of air along a flight route, and forecasts are not promises."},{"p":"Wind can change with time, height, terrain, buildings, and weather systems. Thunderstorms can bring dangerous conditions, including strong gusts and wind shear. Pilots learn to combine multiple sources of information and update decisions as conditions change."},{"p":"**Try this:** With an adult, compare an ordinary weather forecast with the actual conditions outside at three different times. Write down what matched and what didn’t. Don’t assume a difference means the forecast was useless; ask what it was trying to predict."},{"h":"8. The Decision Is Bigger Than the Calculation"},{"p":"Lincoln learns that solving a wind problem on paper is not the same as deciding whether to fly. A correct number is useful, but a safe decision also considers uncertainty, equipment, experience, changing conditions, and the possibility of stopping or choosing another plan."},{"p":"A good pilot doesn’t need to win an argument with the weather. The pilot needs to get everyone home safely."},{"p":"**Lincoln’s Logbook Challenge:** For one week, keep a tiny observation journal. Write the date, time, what you noticed about the wind, and one question you couldn’t answer. At the end of the week, circle the observation that surprised you most. Curiosity is a better starting point than pretending you already know."},{"p":"**For future pilots:** You can begin studying weather, aerodynamics, navigation, and communication long before you’re old enough to fly alone. In the United States, the minimum age to solo a powered airplane is generally sixteen, and to earn an airplane private pilot certificate is generally seventeen, with additional requirements. Lincoln is fourteen, so he remains a supervised student throughout this book."},{"h":"AVIATION NOTE"},{"p":"Aviation procedures, regulations, publications, equipment, and airport information can change. This series is fiction and is not a substitute for flight instruction, current official publications, aircraft operating information, or the judgment of a qualified instructor or pilot in command. Readers interested in learning to fly should use current FAA materials and instruction appropriate to their training."},{"h":"HYPE TEEN FLIGHT ACADEMY"},{"p":"Hype Teen Flight Academy follows Lincoln Burgess and his friends as they grow from curious beginners into young aviators who understand that good flying begins long before takeoff. Across seven books, real aviation concepts become part of stories about friendship, judgment, preparation, setbacks, independence, leadership, and service."},{"p":"1. *Balance Before Control*"},{"p":"2. *Crosswind Decisions*"},{"p":"3. *Weather Minimums*"},{"p":"4. *Cross-Country*"},{"p":"5. *Solo*"},{"p":"6. *Pilot in Command*"},{"p":"7. *The Sky We Share*"}],"links":[{"a":"unit:weather","t":"Weather"},{"a":"unit:landings","t":"Takeoffs + Landings"},{"a":"unit:adm","t":"Decisions + Risk"}]}],"words":31463}); })();
